nbrf bad goals: 0 []\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"source venv/bin/activate && python - <<'EOF'\nimport csv, unicodedata\ncsv.field_size_limit(10**9)\ndef fold(s):\n    s = unicodedata.normalize('NFKD', s)\n    s = ''.join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c))\n    return s.lower().strip()\n\ndef compact(s):\n    return ''.join(ch for ch in fold(s) if ch.isalnum())\n\ndef load(path, enc='utf-8'):\n    with open(path, encoding=enc) as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\nbra = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nnovo = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nbrf = load('data/kaggle/BR-Football-Dataset.csv')\n\n# check overlap novo 2019 vs Brasileirao 2019\ndef sig_bra(r): return (r['season'], compact(r['home_team'].split('-')[0]), compact(r['away_team'].split('-')[0]), r['home_goal'], r['away_goal'])\ndef sig_novo(r): return (r['Ano'], compact(r['Equipe_mandante'].split('-')[0]), compact(r['Equipe_visitante'].split('-')[0]), r['Gols_mandante'], r['Gols_visitante'])\ndef sig_brf(r): return (r['date'][:4], compact(r['home']), compact(r['away']), str(int(float(r['home_goal']))), str(int(float(r['away_goal']))))\n\ns2019_novo = set(sig_novo(r) for r in novo if r['Ano']=='2019')\ns2019_bra = set(sig_bra(r) for r in bra if r['season']=='2019')\ns2019_brf = set(sig_brf(r) for r in brf if r['tournament']=='Serie A' and r['date'][:4]=='2019')\nprint(\"2019 novo:\", len(s2019_novo), \"bra:\", len(s2019_bra), \"brf SerieA:\", len(s2019_brf))\nprint(\"novo∩bra:\", len(s2019_novo & s2019_bra), \"bra∩brf:\", len(s2019_bra & s2019_brf), \"novo∩brf:\", len(s2019_novo & s2019_brf))\nprint(\"bra 2015 vs brf 2015:\", len(set(sig_bra(r) for r in bra if r['season']=='2015') & set(sig_brf(r) for r in brf if r['tournament']=='Serie A' and r['date'][:4]=='2015')))\nprint(\"bra 2022 vs brf 2022:\", len(set(sig_bra(r) for r in bra if r['season']=='2022') & set(sig_brf(r) for r in brf if r['tournament']=='Serie A' and r['date'][:4]=='2022')))\n\n# Copa do Brasil overlap\ncopa = load('data/kaggle/Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv')\ndef sig_copa(r): return (r['season'], compact(r['home_team'].split(' - ')[0].split('-')[0]), r['home_goal'], r['away_goal'])\ns2017_copa = set(sig_copa(r) for r in copa if r['season']=='2017')\ns2017_brf = set(sig_brf(r) for r in brf if r['tournament']=='Copa do Brasil' and r['date'][:4]=='2017')\nprint(\"Copa 2017 file:\", len(s2017_copa), \"brf:\", len(s2017_brf), \"∩:\", len(s2017_copa & s2017_brf))\n\n# Libertadores NA season\nlib = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nna = [r for r in lib if r['season']=='NA' or r['season']=='']\nprint(\"Lib NA rows:\", len(na))\nfor r in na[:5]: print(r)\n# goal NA\nbad = [r for r in lib if not r['home_goal'].replace('.','').isdigit()]\nprint(\"Lib bad goals:\", len(bad), bad[:3])\nbadb = [r for r in bra if not r['home_goal'].isdigit()]\nprint(\"Bra bad goals:\", len(badb))\nbadc = [r for r in copa if not r['home_goal'].isdigit()]\nprint(\"Copa bad goals:\", len(badc), badc[:3])\nbadn = [r for r in novo if not r['Gols_mandante'].isdigit()]\nprint(\"novo bad goals:\", len(badn), badn[:3])\nbadf = [r for r in brf if not r['home_goal'].replace('.','').isdigit()]\nprint(\"brf bad goals:\", len(badf), badf[:3])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788254763010,"end":1788254763015}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good picture. Key normalization challenges:\n\n1. **Brasileirao file**: unaccented with state suffix: \"Atletico-MG\", \"Sao Paulo-SP\", \"Avai-SC\", \"Criciuma-SC\", \"Csa-AL\", \"Vasco da Gama-RJ\", \"America-MG\", \"Atletico-GO\", \"Atletico-PR\".\n2. **Libertadores**: mixed — \"América-MG\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"Athletico\" (bare!), \"Atlético-PR\", \"São Paulo\" (no suffix), \"Botafogo\", \"Vasco\", \"Nacional (URU)\", \"Guaraní (PAR)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Delfín-EQU\" vs \"Delfín\", \"Nacional-URU\" vs \"Nacional (URU)\", \"River Plate-URU\" vs \"River Plate\", \"Libertad-PAR\" vs \"Libertad\", \"Olimpia-PAR\", \"Universitario (PER)\" vs \"Universitario-PER\", \"Independiente Del Valle\" vs \"Independiente del Valle\", \"Trujillanos (VEN)\", \"Newells Old Boys\", \"Rentistas \" (trailing space!).\n   - Note \"Athletico\" bare — this is Athletico Paranaense? Hmm, in 2022 Libertadores, Athletico-PR played. The bare \"Athletico\" is likely Athletico Paranaense. And \"Atlético-PR\" also appears. So both need to map to same key.\n   - \"Vasco\" → Vasco da Gama.\n   - Country suffixes in Libertadores: -EQU, -URU, -PAR, -PER, -VEN, -BOL, -CHI?, -COL? etc. And parenthetical \"(URU)\" etc.\n3. **Copa do Brasil**: \"Name - ST\" format with weird long names, e.g. \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\", \"América - MG\".\n4. **BR-Football**: unaccented, no suffixes: \"Sao Paulo\", \"Flamengo\", \"Athletico Paranaense\"? (it had \"Athletico Paranaense\" I think — let me check: the sample earlier showed \"Athletic Club MG\", \"Athletico Paranaense\", \"Atletico Acreano\"... yes \"Athletico Paranaense\" appears). Also \"America MG\", \"America RN\", \"Botafogo\"? and \"Botafogo PB\"? possibly \"Botafogo-PB\". Also \"Aguia Negra-MS\" (with suffix!), \"ASA AL\"?? Weird ones like \"ASA AL\". Hmm \"ASA AL\" — maybe \"ASA\" from Alagoas. Let me check BR-Football teams more comprehensively — teams like \"Atletico-MG\"? Let me grep. Also check \"Corinthians\" etc. exist.\n5. **novo**: accented with suffix: \"Athletico-PR\", \"Atlético-GO\", \"Atlético-MG\", \"São Paulo\", \"América-MG\", \"Vasco\" (bare in list? yes \"Vasco\"), \"Grêmio Prudente\", \"Barueri\"...\n6. **FIFA**: \"Atlético Mineiro\", \"Atlético Paranaense\", \"Sport Club do Recife\", \"Ceará Sporting Club\", \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\", \"Botafogo\", \"Bahia\", \"Santos\", \"Grêmio\", \"Vitória\", \"Vitória Guimarães\" (Portuguese club! careful — \"Vitória\" bare is Brazilian EC Vitória, \"Vitória Guimarães\" is Portuguese; folded \"vitoriaguimaraes\" ≠ \"vitoria\" so substring match would be an issue if I use substring. Exact key matching avoids this.)\n\nNote: FIFA data does NOT include Flamengo, Palmeiras, Corinthians, São Paulo, Vasco, Cruzeiro... wait it has Cruzeiro. It lacks Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo/Vasco (FIFA 19 didn't have full Brazilian league licensing — those clubs are absent; the Brazilian clubs present have fake players mostly). That's fine — queries like \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" would return \"no players found\" for FIFA... wait, the spec says \"Who are the highest-rated players at Flamengo?\" as an example question. Hmm. FIFA 19 data — let me double check whether Flamengo is in there. From the club list filter above: no Flamengo. OK so answer would be empty; that's a data reality. But wait — the spec example answer shows \"Brazilian players at Brazilian clubs: - Flamengo: 8 players...\" That's aspirational; actual data may differ. Our tools just need to work correctly over the data.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me double-check by grepping the raw CSV for \"Flamengo\".\n\nAlso, Gabriel Barbosa: FIFA data has \"M. Barbosa\"? Gabriel Barbosa was at Inter Milan in FIFA 19... Actually \"Gabriel Barbosa\" doesn't exist; there's \"G. Barbosa\"? The search found 'M. Barbosa' Villarreal, 'Hélder Barbosa', 'Barbosaldinho'. Fine — name search tool will return whatever matches.\n\nNow — cross-file queries: \"player + match data\" e.g. \"Which players play for Flamengo?\" → filter FIFA by club. Since FIFA lacks Flamengo, the cross-file aspect works via e.g. Grêmio, Santos, etc.\n\n**Design**:\n\nPackage layout (flat-ish in current dir as instructed — \"Write all code files to the current directory\"; subdirectory for a package is still \"in the current directory\" in spirit, but let me keep it simple: a package dir `brazilian_soccer/` plus top-level `server.py` entry, and `tests/`.)\n\nHmm, \"Write all code files to the current directory\" — I'll create:\n- `server.py` — MCP server entry point (FastMCP)\n- `brazilian_soccer/` package:\n  - `__init__.py`\n  - `models.py` — dataclasses: Match, Player, TeamRecord/StandingsRow\n  - `normalize.py` — team name normalization + alias table + fold\n  - `loader.py` — loads all 6 CSVs into memory (SoccerData)\n  - `analysis.py` — query/analysis functions (match search, h2h, standings, team stats, player search, etc.)\n- `tests/` — BDD tests with pytest-bdd (.feature files + step defs) + unit tests\n- `requirements.txt`\n- `README.md` update? The task says read TASK.md and implement; README update is good practice (the prompts.txt sample mentions updating README, and it's harmless). I'll update README.md to document the server.\n\nWait — should I use pytest-bdd? It gives true Gherkin BDD. The spec says \"Use BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) test scenarios\" with Gherkin examples. pytest-bdd is the natural fit. Let me install it. Also pytest is already there. Let me install pytest-bdd.\n\nAlternatively, write GWT-structured tests with plain pytest using classes/comments. pytest-bdd is more faithful to \"BDD Gherkin\". Let me install pytest-bdd (needs parse, typing? small).\n\n**MCP server design** (FastMCP from `mcp.server.fastmcp`):\n\nTools (each returns formatted text, like the spec's answer formats):\n1. `search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20)` — returns list of matches with date, score, competition, round/stage.\n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition=None)` — matches + W/D/L summary from both perspectives.\n3. `team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None)` — matches, W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate.\n4. `search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, sort_by='overall', limit=20)` — player list.\n5. `club_players(club, sort_by='overall', limit=20)` — maybe merge into search_players; keep separate for \"highest-rated at club\". Actually search_players(club=...) covers it. I'll have `top_players(club=?, nationality=?, limit=)`. Hmm, simpler: one `search_players` with `sort_by` and `limit`. And `player_lookup(name)` returns full detail (all attributes) for a single player.\n6. `standings(competition, season)` — computed table for Brasileirão (or Serie B from BR-Football?) — points, W/D/L, GF/GA, GD, champion, relegated (bottom 4).\n7. `champion(competition, season)` — winner of league (top of table) or cup (winner of final).\n8. `finals(competition, season)` — final matches of cup competitions (Copa do Brasil last round, Libertadores \"final\" stage).\n9. `biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` — largest goal margins.\n10. `average_goals(competition=None, season=None)` — goals/match, home win %, etc.\n11. `home_away_split(competition=None, season=None)` — home vs away performance.\n12. `best_records(venue='home'|'away', competition=None, season=None, limit=10)` — best home/away record teams.\n13. `derbies(season=None, competition=None)` — known derby pairs (Fla-Flu, Majestoso, Choque-Rei, Grenal, Ba-Vi, Clássico Vovô, etc.) matches.\n14. `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)` — most recent match + score.\n15. `team_competitions(team)` — which competitions/files a team appears in, with match counts.\n16. `compare_seasons(season_a, season_b, competition=None)` — aggregate stats across two seasons (total goals, avg, home win rate, biggest win, top team by points?).\n17. `top_scorers`? Not inferable per-player from match data — spec says \"Top scorers (if inferable from data)\" — not inferable; skip (document that).\n18. `list_competitions()` — available competitions and seasons coverage. Nice for discovery.\n19. `search_teams(query)` — resolve team name variations, show canonical + variants + match counts. Helps \"Handles team name variations correctly\".\n\nThat's plenty to answer 20+ sample questions.\n\nAlso a `resources`? MCP resources could expose competition list. Keep it simple: tools only, maybe a resource for dataset info. FastMCP supports `@mcp.resource`. I'll add a small resource for dataset summary. Optional but nice.\n\n**Match model fields**: id, date (date), time?, competition (canonical name), season, stage/round, home_team_key, home_display, away_team_key, away_display, home_goals, away_goals, stadium, source file, extra stats (corners, shots, attacks, ht scores).\n\n**Competitions canonical names**:\n- \"Brasileirão\" (from Brasileirao_Matches.csv + novo 2003-2019 → \"Brasileirão\") — same competition, different eras. Merge both into competition \"Brasileirão\"? They're both Serie A. novo covers 2003-2019, Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2022. Overlap 2012-2019 — duplicates! Same matches in both files (e.g., 2012 Palmeiras vs Portuguesa... novo has 2012 too). Merging naively → double-counting in standings for 2012-2019 seasons.\n\nHmm! This is a real data-quality issue. Options:\n(a) Keep them as separate competition entries: \"Brasileirão (2012-2022)\" and \"Brasileirão Histórico (2003-2019)\". Ugly for queries like \"who won 2019 Brasileirão\" — ambiguous.\n(b) Deduplicate: merge, and for overlapping seasons pick one source per season (prefer Brasileirao_Matches for 2012+ since it has round numbers and cleaner data; novo for 2003-2011). Dedup by (season, date, home_key, away_key) — safest: build set of (season, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals) and skip exact dupes? But two different matches same season same pairing same score could happen (home/away legs are distinct because home/away differs; same pairing+score twice in a season is possible but rare... actually in Serie A each pair plays twice, once each venue, so (season, home, away) is unique except abandoned/replayed games).\n\nCleanest: when building the unified match list, dedupe on (date, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals) first, then on (season, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals)? The second is risky. Let me do: merge with preferred-source ordering per season:\n- For seasons 2012-2019 present in both files, use Brasileirao_Matches rows only (skip novo rows for those seasons), BUT keep novo rows for 2003-2011.\n- Wait, but do they actually contain the same matches? novo 2012-2019 vs Brasileirao_Matches 2012-2019. Both cover Serie A fully (380 matches/season). Let me verify counts: Brasileirao_Matches.csv = 4180 rows for 2012-2022 (11 seasons). 11*380 = 4180. Full coverage. novo = 6886 for 2003-2019 (17 seasons): 2003-2005 had different formats (2003: 46 games? Actually 2003 had 24 teams... whatever), 2006-2019 standard 380 except 2016 (27 teams?)... Actually 2020 had 20 teams = 380. Let me not verify precisely; approach: per season, if season in both files, prefer the primary file (Brasileirao_Matches); else use novo. And also, when both available, mark the competition the same \"Brasileirão\".\n\nActually — wait. Are they really duplicates? Let me check a specific match: 2012-05-19 Palmeiras vs Portuguesa 1-1 (Brasileirao_Matches round 1). novo 2012 round 1 should have \"Palmeiras\" vs \"Portuguesa\" 1-1 on 19/05/2012. Let me verify in code. If they match, dedup approach works.\n\nAlso BR-Football-Dataset \"Serie A\" overlaps 2014-2023 with Brasileirao_Matches 2014-2022! Same competition again! Serie A rows = 3689. BR-Football date range 2014-2023. Brasileirao_Matches covers 2012-2022. Overlap 2014-2022. Ugh.\n\nSo for \"Brasileirão\" I have THREE sources: Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022), novo (2003-2019), BR-Football Serie A (2014-2023).\n\nApproach: unified competition \"Brasileirão Serie A\" with per-season source preference:\n1. Brasileirao_Matches (most authoritative for 2012-2022, has rounds)\n2. novo for 2003-2011\n3. BR-Football \"Serie A\" for 2023 only (only source with 2023)\n\nBut BR-Football has extra stats (corners, shots) — for match-detail queries those are valuable. I can attach extra stats to matches by joining on (date, home, away) — the BR-Football teams are unaccented (\"Sao Paulo\") but fold to same keys. Dates: Brasileirao datetime \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" vs BR-Football date \"2023-09-24\" — join on date part.\n\nPlan: \n- Load all sources into raw match lists.\n- Build canonical match list per competition with source preference, dedup by (date, home_key, away_key) — exact dupes. For (season, home, away) with different dates (rescheduled), could double count... Let me check duplicates empirically and decide.\n- Enrich matches with stats from BR-Football where joinable.\n\nSimilarly \"Copa do Brasil\" exists in Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv (2012-2021) AND BR-Football \"Copa do Brasil\" (1123 rows, 2014-2023?). Preference: Brazilian_Cup_Matches for 2012-2021, BR-Football for 2022-2023. Dedup.\n\nAnd \"Libertadores\" only from Libertadores_Matches.csv (no other source). BR-Football has Serie B and Serie C which no other file has → competitions \"Brasileirão Serie B\" (2014-2023), \"Brasileirão Serie C\".\n\nWait, should Serie B be \"Brasileirão Serie B\"? It's usually called \"Série B\". Competition names:\n- \"Brasileirão\" (Serie A merged) — display \"Brasileirão Serie A\"\n- \"Serie B\", \"Serie C\" from BR-Football\n- \"Copa do Brasil\" (merged)\n- \"Copa Libertadores\" (Libertadores file) — note it includes non-Brazilian teams.\n\nLet me verify overlap duplicates empirically before deciding dedup keys. Actually, let me think about what could go wrong with \"prefer one source per season\": if source A is missing some matches that source B has for the same season, we'd lose them. Safer: for each season, use preferred source; if preferred source has 0 matches for that season, fall back to others. Additionally, union of matches not in preferred source for a season? Could double-count rescheduled matches with changed dates. Risk either way. \n\nBetter dedup strategy: within a competition, dedup on (season, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals, round-ish)? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at data reality: do the two Brasileirão sources for the same season have identical match sets? Let me check 2015 season: Brasileirao_Matches 2015 vs novo 2015 vs BR-Football Serie A 2015. If match sets are near-identical, prefer-one-source is right and lossless.\n\nLet me test empirically. Also check Copa do Brasil overlap.\n\nAlso for standings, I should use one source per season (else double-counted points). For general match search, dedup by (date, home, away, score) is fine.\n\nActually — simplest robust approach that serves both standings and search:\n- Per competition per season, choose a single source (preference order), and if the chosen source lacks the season entirely, use next source that has it. This guarantees no double counting and full coverage. Matches enriched with BR-Football stats when the same (date, home, away) exists there.\n\nEdge: BR-Football Serie A 2023 gives us 2023 Brasileirão. Good — spec examples reference 2023 (\"What matches did Palmeiras play in 2023?\"). \n\nLet me now verify empirically. Then design the standings: only for league competitions (Brasileirão, Serie B, Serie C) — 3 pts/win. Copa/Libertadores: cup format; standings don't make sense; instead \"finals\" query. \"Which teams were relegated in 2020?\" — bottom 4 of Brasileirão table.\n\nHalf-time results: BR-Football ht_result/at_result are from each team's perspective (\"DRAW\",\"WON\",\"LOST\"). For match detail output.\n\nCorners/shots/attacks: match detail.\n\nNow the **date parsing**:\n- \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" ISO with time\n- \"2023-09-24\" ISO date\n- \"29/03/2003\" Brazilian DD/MM/YYYY\n- Libertadores season \"NA\" rows — season missing. Handle: season=None, and competition queries by season skip them... or infer season from date? If datetime exists, infer season from year. Let me check those NA rows — do they have datetime? Probably. Infer season = year of date.\n\nAlso check for missing goals (\"NA\"?) — Libertadores goals are strings \"2\". Some may be \"NA\". Handle: skip matches without scores (can't compute stats) but keep? If goals missing, exclude from stats but could still show as scheduled? Simpler: skip rows with unparseable goals/date — count and log. Let me check for NA values.\n\n**Team canonical registry** — I'll build:\n\n```python\nCANONICAL_TEAMS = {\n    # key: (display, state)\n    \"flamengo\": (\"Flamengo\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"fluminense\": (\"Fluminense\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"vascodagama\": (\"Vasco da Gama\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"botafogo\": (\"Botafogo\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"botafogopb\": (\"Botafogo-PB\", \"PB\"),\n    \"corinthians\": (\"Corinthians\", \"SP\"),\n    \"palmeiras\": (\"Palmeiras\", \"SP\"),\n    \"saopaulo\": (\"São Paulo\", \"SP\"),\n    \"santos\": (\"Santos\", \"SP\"),\n    \"gremio\": (\"Grêmio\", \"RS\"),\n    \"internacional\": (\"Internacional\", \"RS\"),\n    \"juventude\": (\"Juventude\", \"RS\"),\n    \"criciuma\": (\"Criciúma\", \"SC\"),\n    \"atleticomineiro\": (\"Atlético Mineiro\", \"MG\"),\n    \"cruzeiro\": (\"Cruzeiro\", \"MG\"),\n    \"americamineiro\": (\"América Mineiro\", \"MG\"),\n    \"athleticoparanaense\": (\"Athletico Paranaense\", \"PR\"),\n    \"atleticoparanaense\": (\"Athletico Paranaense\", \"PR\"),  # legacy spelling\n    \"atleticogoianiense\": (\"Atlético Goianiense\", \"GO\"),\n    \"goias\": (\"Goiás\", \"GO\"),\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nWith alias mapping from every observed variant → key. The fold of canonical display gives the key (e.g. \"Atlético Mineiro\" → \"atleticomineiro\"). Aliases: \"atleticomg\" → \"atleticomineiro\", \"atletico-go\"? fold → \"atleticogo\" hmm — \"Atlético-GO\" folds to \"atletico go\" → normalized (remove non-alnum) \"atleticogo\". Alias \"atleticogo\" → \"atleticogoianiense\". \"Atletico-GO\" from Brasileirao file folds to \"atleticogo\" (unaccented). novo \"Atlético-GO\" folds same. \n\nAlias table (folded-variant → canonical key):\n- \"atleticomg\" → \"atleticomineiro\"\n- \"america\" → \"americamineiro\"?? DANGER: América-RN and América-CE/SC exist. \"América - MG\" (Copa) → fold+strip state → \"america\" ambiguous!\n\nUgh. OK here's the thing: \"América\" is genuinely ambiguous. In the Copa file, \"América - MG\" and \"América - RN\" both appear. If I alias folded-full-names INCLUDING suffix BEFORE stripping, I can distinguish: \"americamg\" → americamineiro, \"americarn\" → \"americarn\". The fallback strip-rule (\"América - MG\" → \"America\") is only used when no full-name alias matches. So I need full-name-with-suffix aliases for ambiguous clubs:\n\nAMBIGUOUS = {atleticomg/atleticogo/atleticopr/athletico, americamg/americarn/americace/americasc, botafogo/botafogopb, nacional..., internacional vs inter? }\n\nBut wait — \"America-MG\" (Brasileirao) folds to \"americamg\"? fold(\"America-MG\") = \"america-mg\" → remove punctuation → \"americamg\". Yes if I strip the hyphen. And \"América - MG\" → \"amrica mg\"? No — fold strips accent: \"America - MG\" → \"america - mg\" → remove non-alnum+collapse → \"americamg\". Both → \"americamg\". \n\nSo the algorithm:\n```\ndef team_key(raw):\n    name = strip_parenthetical(raw).strip()\n    fk = fold_compact(name)   # lowercase, unaccent, remove non-alnum\n    if fk in ALIASES: return ALIASES[fk]\n    stripped = strip_state_suffix(name)  # remove trailing \"-XX\"/\" - XX\" 2-3 letter code\n    fk2 = fold_compact(stripped)\n    if fk2 in ALIASES: return ALIASES[fk2]\n    return fk2  # generic\n```\n\nALIASES (fold_compact form → canonical key):\n- \"atleticomg\" → \"atleticomineiro\"; \"atleticogo\" → \"atleticogoianiense\"; \"atleticopr\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"; \"athleticopr\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"; \"athletico\" → \"athleticoparanaense\"; \"atlético-pr\"... folded same.\n- \"americamg\" → \"americamineiro\"; \"americarn\" → \"americarn\"; \"americace\" → \"americace\"; \"americasc\"→...\n- \"botafogopb\" → \"botafogopb\"; plain \"botafogo\" → \"botafogo\".\n- \"sport\" → \"sportrecife\"; \"sportpe\" → \"sportrecife\"; \"sportclubdorecife\" → \"sportrecife\".\n- \"ceara\" → \"ceara\"; \"cearasportingclub\" → \"ceara\".\n- \"vasco\" → \"vascodagama\".\n- \"saopaulo\" → \"saopaulo\"; \"saopaulofc\" → \"saopaulo\".\n- \"corinthians\": \"sportclubcorinthianspaulista\" → \"corinthians\".\n- \"gremio\": \"gremiofbpa\"? not in data. \"gremioprudente\" → keep distinct (it's a different club! Grêmio Prudente 2010). fold(\"Grêmio Prudente\")=\"gremioprudente\" — distinct key, fine.\n- \"internacional\": \"inter\"? not used alone in data. OK.\n- \"nautico\" → \"nautico\".\n- \"parana\" → \"parana\". \"paranaclube\"? not present.\n- \"pontepreta\" → \"pontepreta\".\n- \"redbullbragantino\" → \"redbullbragantino\". Brasileirao has \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" → strip → \"redbullbragantino\". Good.\n- \"csa\" → \"csa\" (Centro Sportivo Alagoano). \n- \"avai\" → \"avai\". \"figueirense\" → \"figueirense\". \"chapecoense\" → \"chapecoense\".\n- \"cuiaba\" → \"cuiaba\" (Cuiabá-MT).\n- \"jo inville\" → \"joinville\".\n- \"santacruz\" → \"santacruz\". \"santoandre\" → \"santoandre\". \"saocaetano\" → \"saocaetano\". \"barueri\" → \"barueri\". \"guarani\" → \"guarani\" (careful: Guarani SP vs Guarani-CG? In novo, \"Guarani\" is Campinas. Libertadores \"Guaraní (PAR)\" is Paraguayan → fold \"guaranipar\"... \"Guaraní (PAR)\" strip parenthetical → \"Guaraní\" → \"guarani\"!! COLLISION with Brazilian Guarani. Need state/country-aware: \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → fold_compact(\"Guaraní (PAR)\") = \"guaranipar\" → alias \"guaranipar\" → \"guarani(paraguay)\" hmm. But wait, my algorithm strips parentheticals BEFORE checking aliases. \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → strip paren → \"Guaraní\" → \"guarani\" — collision with Brazilian Guarani!\n\nFix: check alias table on fold_compact of the FULL raw name (with parentheses removed but suffix kept)? \"Guaraní (PAR)\" → removing only the parens chars but keeping content: \"Guaraní PAR\" → fold \"guaranipar\" → alias → Paraguayan club. That works! So: alias check on fold_compact(name with parens stripped of bracket chars only, content retained). \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacionaluru\" → alias → nacional Uruguay. \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" (FIFA) → \"americafcminasgerais\" → alias → americamineiro. \n\nBut \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → parens content retained → \"boavistasportclubantigoesporteclubebarreirarj\" — no alias → fallback: strip trailing state \"- RJ\"... the compacted form ends with \"rj\" — my strip_state works on the spaced name \"Boavista Sport Club (antigo Esporte Clube Barreira) - RJ\" → need to remove the parenthetical CONTENT for the fallback path (it's noise). So two-stage:\n1. compact_full = fold_compact(name-with-parens-chars-removed) → alias lookup (handles \"Guaraní (PAR)\", \"Nacional (URU)\", \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\", \"Barcelona-EQU\", \"Delfín-EQU\", \"Nacional-URU\" → \"nacionaluru\" ✓).\n2. If no hit: name2 = remove parenthetical content entirely; strip trailing state/country code (2-3 uppercase letters after \"-\"); compact and alias-check again (handles \"Boavista ... - RJ\" → \"boavista\"? wait — strip suffix \"RJ\": \"Boavista Sport Club\" → \"boavistasportclub\" — key stays long. Could also strip trailing \"sport club\"/\"fc\"? For display we keep raw-ish. Hmm, display name for such teams: use the stripped name. OK.)\n3. If still no hit: return compact as key.\n\nAlso trailing spaces (\"Rentistas \") — strip.\n\nNow state stripping regex on spaced name: `[-–]\\s*([A-Za-z]{2,3})$` — e.g. \"Palmeiras-SP\" → \"Palmeiras\"; \"América - MG\" → \"América\"; \"Barcelona-EQU\" → \"Barcelona\"; \"Nacional-URU\" → \"Nacional\". Also \"ASA AL\"? BR-Football has \"ASA AL\" (space-separated state). Hmm: \"ASA AL\" → strip \" AL\" suffix? Add pattern `\\s+([A-Z]{2})$` for uppercase pairs? \"America MG\" (BR-Football) → \"America\". \"Aguia Negra-MS\". Let me handle: trailing token of 2-3 letters that is ALL-CAPS in the original OR is a known state/country code → strip. Since fold lowercases everything for the key, do the strip BEFORE folding on the raw spaced name: check regex `[-\\s]\\s*[A-Z]{2,3}$` on the raw (after paren-removal). Careful: \"Red Bull Bragantino-SP\" ends \"-SP\" ✓ strip. \"Sport Boys\" (Peruvian club!) ends \" Boys\" — not all-caps → safe. \"CSA\" — the whole name is \"CSA\", ends with \"A\" single char — safe. \"ASA AL\": ends \" AL\" caps → strip → \"ASA\" ✓. \"LDU\" — whole name caps 3 letters: pattern requires separator before the code, \"LDU\" has no separator → safe ✓. \"ABC\" safe similarly. \"EC Vitória\"? ends \"Vitória\" — safe. \"4 de Julho EC\" ends \"EC\" caps! → would strip \" EC\" → \"4 de Julho\". Hmm — \"4 de Julho EC\" (Piauí club). Stripping \"EC\" (estádio? no, \"Esporte Clube\") — actually that's DESIRABLE-ish (\"4 de Julho\") but \"EC\" isn't a state. It'd map to \"4dejulho\". Is there another \"4 de Julho\" variant? BR-Football also has... let me check if both \"4 de Julho EC\" and \"4 de Julho\" exist. If both exist, stripping unifies them — good actually. But \"Botafogo EC\"? \"Botafogo-PB\"? Hmm \"Botafogo EC\" might be the Paraíba one in BR-Football (\"Botafogo PB\"?). Earlier team list from BR-Football sample showed only up to \"Atletico Acreano\". Let me get the full BR-Football team list and check tricky cases.\n\nAlso country codes: EQU (Ecuador), URU, PAR, PER, VEN, BOL, CHI, COL, ARG?, MEX? — the suffix pattern [A-Z]{2,3} handles them. \"Guaraní-PAR\" → \"guaranipar\" via stage-1 compact alias (not needed — stage 2 strips \"-PAR\" → \"Guarani\"... wait stage 1 runs first on compacted full: \"guaranipar\" — if I have alias \"guaranipar\" → paraguayan guarani, great. Otherwise stage 2 strips \"-PAR\" → \"guarani\" → collides with Brazilian Guarani. So I MUST add aliases for these Libertadores foreign suffixed variants. There are a limited number: Guaraní (PAR), Guaraní-PAR, Nacional (URU), Nacional-URU, Nacional (PAR), Barcelona-EQU, Delfín-EQU, Olimpia-PAR, Libertad-PAR, River Plate-URU, Universitario (PER), Universitario-PER, Trujillanos (VEN), Sport Boys?, \"Ind. Santa Fe\"...\n\nHmm, but do I actually CARE about disambiguating Paraguayan Guaraní