eam key → matches list, for speed. Simple dict indexes: matches_by_team, matches_by_competition, players_by_club_key. All queries < 5s trivially.\n\n**MCP server**: FastMCP over stdio. Entry `server.py`. Test the MCP layer via `mcp.client` in-memory (session) — mcp package provides `ClientSession` and `create_client_session`? In mcp 2.1.1: `from mcp import ClientSession, StdioServerParameters; from mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client`. I can spawn the server as subprocess and test tools end-to-end. That's a solid integration test. Let me confirm the API in the installed version.\n\nAlso the guide says \"knowledge graph interface\" — I'll present entities/relations in output (e.g., team profiles listing competitions, matches as relations). I'll mention in README that data is modeled as an entity graph (teams, players, matches, competitions) exposed via MCP tools.\n\n**BDD tests**: pytest-bdd with .feature files matching the spec's Gherkin. Categories: match queries, team queries, player queries, competition queries, statistical analysis, team-name normalization, MCP server integration, performance. I'll write feature files with scenarios covering the spec's examples + step definitions + conftest with fixtures (loaded data, MCP client session).\n\nInstall pytest-bdd now. Also I should pin requirements.txt: mcp>=1.0, pandas? — I decided stdlib csv! Wait, I installed pandas but do I need it? Let me go stdlib csv — fewer moving parts (pandas 3.0 just dropped; avoids API risk). Actually for the FIFA file with 80+ columns, csv.DictReader handles it easily. Yes: **stdlib csv only** for loading. Requirements: mcp, pytest, pytest-bdd. (pandas unnecessary — leave it installed but don't require it.)\n\nHmm, one consideration: \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass\" — I'll run pytest at the end.\n\n**Half-time result columns** ht_result/at_result: per-team perspective WON/LOST/DRAW. I'll store raw strings and also ht goals? ht_diff/at_diff are... \"ht_result, at_result | Half-time results\" — values DRAW/WON/LOST per team. There are also ht_diff, at_diff columns (floats: -1.0, 1.0, 0.0). I'll store as strings.\n\nWait, looking at BR-Football columns: `time,date,ht_diff,at_diff,ht_result,at_result` — the header order in the CSV: `home_corner,away_corner,home_attack,away_attack,home_shots,away_shots,time,date,ht_diff,at_diff,ht_result,at_result,total_corners`. Note the spec said \"ht_result, at_result | Half-time results\" — and sample row: `ht_diff=0.0, at_diff=0.0, ht_result=DRAW, at_result=DRAW`. Hmm ht_diff/at_diff probably half-time goal diff from each team's perspective. Whatever — store what's there for match detail output.\n\n**Detailed tool list** (final):\n\n1. `search_matches(team, opponent, competition, season, date_from, date_to, stage, limit)` \n2. `head_to_head(team_a, team_b, competition, limit)`\n3. `last_match_between(team_a, team_b)`\n4. `team_stats(team, season, competition, venue)` — venue: home/away/any\n5. `team_profile(team)` — competitions played, seasons, totals, W/D/L, GF/GA + variants of name (knowledge-graph-ish)\n6. `search_players(name, nationality, club, position, min_overall, max_overall, sort_by, ascending, limit)`\n7. `player_details(name)` — full attributes for best name match(es)\n8. `standings(competition, season, full_table)` — league tables; marks champion & relegated (bottom 4)\n9. `competition_finals(competition, season)` — final-round matches of cup competitions (Copa do Brasil: highest round; Libertadores: stage == 'final'); returns champion\n10. `champion(competition, season)` — league: top points; cup: winner of finals\n11. `biggest_wins(competition, season, limit)` \n12. `average_goals(competition, season)` — avg goals/match, home win %, draw %, away win %\n13. `home_away_split(competition, season)` — home vs away aggregate performance\n14. `best_records(venue, competition, season, min_matches, limit)` — best home/away records by win rate\n15. `derbies(season, competition, limit)` — known Brazilian derby matches\n16. `team_competitions(team)` — per-competition appearance counts (merges team_profile? team_profile includes this; keep team_competitions separate)\n17. `compare_seasons(season_a, season_b, competition)` \n18. `list_competitions()` — competitions with season coverage + team counts\n19. `search_teams(query)` — fuzzy team name resolution\n\nThat's 19 tools — great coverage for \"at least 20 sample questions\".\n\nPlus resource: `soccer://datasets` maybe. Keep tools only + maybe one resource. FastMCP: `@mcp.resource(\"data://datasets\")`. I'll add it — cheap.\n\n**Output format**: match the spec's example answer formats (human-readable text blocks).\n\nNow, before writing code, empirically verify:\n1. novo vs Brasileirao_Matches vs BR-Football Serie A overlap (same matches?).\n2. Copa do Brasil vs BR-Football Copa do Brasil overlap.\n3. Libertadores \"NA\" season rows.\n4. Missing/odd values in goals (NA strings).\n5. Full BR-Football team list + full Copa team list (for alias curation).\n6. FIFA position values (ST, LW, ... GK), and club \"Flamengo\" absence.\n7. Check mcp 2.1.1 FastMCP + client testing API.\n\nLet me run these checks.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c4a1f30001Ow4djsNFi33Tc1","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c48000d001F7dzTGQS5Mwlkk"}}
