son, dict(sorted(rounds.items())))\nlib = load('data/kaggle/Libertadores_Matches.csv')\nfinals = Counter(r['season'] for r in lib if r['stage']=='final')\nprint(\"lib finals/season:\", dict(finals))\n\n# Where do Vitoria EC / Fortaleza FC / Botafogo SP appear?\nfor name in ['Vitoria EC','Fortaleza FC','Botafogo SP','Internacional - SC','Central SC','Atletico - PR','Athletico','America RN','Nacional - AM']:\n    srcs = Counter((r['tournament'], r['date'][:4]) for r in brf if r['home']==name or r['away']==name)\n    print(name, \"->\", dict(srcs))\n\n# bra internal dup check\nbra = load('data/kaggle/Brasileirao_Matches.csv')\nfor season in ['2019','2018']:\n    c = Counter((r['datetime'], r['home_team'], r['away_team']) for r in bra if r['season']==season)\n    dups = {k:v for k,v in c.items() if v>1}\n    print(\"bra\", season, \"dup datetime+teams:\", dups)\nc2 = Counter((r['home_team'], r['away_team'], r['home_goal'], r['away_goal']) for r in bra if r['season']=='2019')\nprint(\"bra 2019 dup pairing+score:\", {k:v for k,v in c2.items() if v>1})\n\n# novo vs bra for 2019: check if identical match set\ndef parse_novo_date(s):\n    from datetime import datetime\n    return datetime.strptime(s, '%d/%m/%Y').date()\nnovo = load('data/kaggle/novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv')\nn2019 = set((str(parse_novo_date(r['Data'])), r['Equipe_mandante'].replace('-PR','').replace('-MG','').replace('-GO',''), r['Equipe_visitante'].split('-')[0]) for r in novo if r['Ano']=='2019')\nb2019 = set((r['datetime'][:10], r['home_team'].split('-')[0], r['away_team'].split('-')[0]) for r in bra if r['season']=='2019')\nprint(\"novo2019:\", len(n2019), \"bra2019:\", len(b2019), \"∩:\", len(n2019 & b2019))\nEOF","time":{"start":1788255015199,"end":1788255015205}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Excellent! This confirms the design. Key insights:\n\n1. **Series B 2021 has 446 rows with no exact duplicates and no same-date+teams duplicates** — 424 unique pairings. So Serie B 2021 in BR-Football genuinely has 446 distinct rows?? Serie B 2021 had 20 teams = 380 games. 446 - 380 = 66 extra. Pairings: 424 unique (home,away) direction pairs — 380 would be the number of (home,away) directed pairings for 20 teams double round-robin... wait: 20 teams, each plays 38 games, total 380 matches. Each ordered pairing (A home vs B) appears exactly once. So unique directed pairings should be 380, not 424. So there are 44 pairing-duplicates with different dates. Possibly some matches recorded twice with different dates (postponed matches recorded at both dates?) or... maybe 2021 Serie B rows include REPLAYED matches or the dataset double counts some rounds. Hmm. Actually wait — maybe BR-Football \"Serie B\" 2021 includes BOTH the regular season AND something else? Or maybe my year extraction from date is wrong (some matches from 2022 season dated 2021?). Serie B 2021 ran May-Nov 2021. 446...\n\nWhatever the cause, for standings I should dedupe by (home, away) pairing per season: if a directed pairing appears more than once in a season for a league competition, keep... hmm, which one? This is getting deep. Practical approach: for standings, group by pairing and keep the LAST (most recent date) match — as that's likely the actually-played one. Or keep first. Can't know. Actually — wait. Let me look: are these maybe duplicated across two half-seasons? Let me just check a pairing that appears twice.\n\nActually, let me not over-engineer. Options:\n(a) For standings: per (season, home, away) keep the match with the latest date. Rationale: postponed matches get rescheduled to a later date; the row with the later date is the final result. But if the dataset erroneously has two different results... whatever, it's noisy data; pick latest.\n\nHmm wait, actually let me check: maybe BR-Football 2021 Serie B rows include matches from calendar-2021 that belong to the 2020 season (played in 2021 due to COVID)! Serie B 2020 season ran Sep 2020 - Jan 2021. So calendar-year grouping by match date mis-buckets Jan-2021 matches of the 2019/2020 season into \"2021\". THAT's the overlap issue: \"2020\": 302 rows + early 2021 rows belonging to 2020 season = 302+X. And 2021: 446 = 380 (2021 season) + 66 (tail of 2020 season played in Jan 2021)! Similarly Serie A 2021: 491 = 380 + 111 (Serie A 2020 season ended Jan/Feb 2021? Serie A 2020 ended Feb 24 2021 — hmm 491-380=111, plausible late-2020-season matches played Jan-Feb 2021). And Serie A 2020 = 264 + ~111... = ~375 ≈ 380. YES — that's it. The BR-Football dataset bucketed by CALENDAR year, and COVID shifted the 2020 seasons into early 2021.\n\nHow to fix: for league competitions in BR-Football, bucket by SEASON not calendar year. Can I infer season? A heuristic: Brazilian Serie A/B seasons run roughly April-December. Matches in Jan-March belong to the PREVIOUS season. So: for BR-Football league matches (Serie A, B, C): if match month is Jan-Mar (1-3), assign season = year - 1, else season = year. Let me verify: Serie A 2021 491 rows — how many are dated Jan-Mar 2021? If ~111, that confirms. Then 2020: 264 + those 111 = 375. And Copa do Brasil 2021=111: Copa 2021 ran June-Nov 2021; Copa 2020 final was Mar/Apr 2021 (Corinthians?). Hmm Copa 2020 semifinal/final were played in early 2021! So Copa do Brasil also needs the same adjustment. And 2022/2023 Copas end in Oct/Nov, no bleed. Let me verify empirically: count BR-Football rows by tournament × (year, month bucket).\n\n2. **Team alias structure confirmed**: groups show exactly the variants I predicted. Notable:\n   - \"atletico\" group: \"Atletico - ES\", \"Atletico - PR\"(!), \"Atletico-GO\", \"Atletico-MG\" — WAIT. \"Atletico - PR\" appears?! In COPA file? \"Atletico - PR\" — hmm from the group: ['Atletico - ES', 'Atletico - PR', 'Atletico-GO', 'Atletico-MG', 'Atletico-PR', 'Atlético - AC', 'Atlético - BA', 'Atlético - GO', 'Atlético - MG', 'Atlético - PR']. So Copa do Brasil has \"Atlético - MG\" and \"Atlético - PR\" and \"Atlético - BA\" (Bahia's Atlético de Alagoinhas!) and \"Atlético - AC\" (Acre). ALL map to compact \"atletico\" after state strip. These are DIFFERENT clubs — must disambiguate WITH state for those. My planned full-name-with-suffix aliases handle: \"atléticomg\"→atleticomineiro, \"atléticopr\"→athleticoparanaense, \"atléticoba\"→atleticodealagoinhas, \"atléticoac\"→atleticoacreano, \"atléticoes\"→atleticoes? (ES = Espírito Santo club). So the alias table must include these state-suffixed Atlético/America/Botafogo/Santa Cruz etc. variants. The generic strip rule is only fallback for names NOT in the alias table.\n   - \"botafogo\" group: Botafogo - PB, Botafogo - RJ, Botafogo SP (Botafogo-SP? There's Botafogo SP = Ribeirão Preto! \"Botafogo SP\" vs \"Botafogo PB\" vs \"Botafogo RJ\"). So aliases: \"botafogorj\"→botafogo (the big RJ one), \"botafogosp\"→botafogosp (Botafogo-SP Ribeirão Preto), \"botafogopb\"→botafogopb.\n   - \"santacruz\": Santa Cruz - PE (big one), Santa Cruz - RN, Santa Cruz - RS! → aliases with state.\n   - \"santos\": Santos - SP (big), Santos - AP (Santos-AP = Independente? there's a \"Santos - AP\" club (Macapá)). → aliases.\n   - \"nacional\": Nacional (PAR), Nacional (URU), Nacional - AM (Brazilian Amazonas), Nacional-URU → aliases: \"nacionaluru\"→nacionaluru, \"nacionalpar\"→nacionalpar, \"nacionalam\"→nacionalam.\n   - \"saoraimundo\", \"saoluiz\", \"juventude\" (Juventude-RS big vs Juventude-MA!), \"fluminense\" (RJ big vs Fluminense PI vs \"Fluminense De Feira\" separate group ✓), \"flamengo\" (RJ big vs \"Flamengo - PI\"!), \"internacional\" (RS big vs Internacional - SC!), \"operario\" (PR/MS/MT), \"river\" (PI vs AC), \"ypiranga\" (AP vs RS), \"vitoria\" (BA big vs Vitoria EC/ES?), \"globo\" (RN), \"real\" (RR), \"rio branco\" (AC vs ES), \"guarani\" (SP vs CE vs PAR's Guaraní), \"america\" (MG/RN/CE...).\n   \n   So the rule: for ambiguous base names, the STATE-SUFFIXED compact form is the disambiguating alias, mapping to a canonical key. For the BIG club of each name, canonical = the base name (e.g., \"flamengo\", \"botafogo\", \"santacruz\", \"fluminense\", \"internacional\", \"juventude\", \"santos\", \"guarani\"? hmm guarani-SP is \"Guarani\" (Campinas)...).\n\n   For Copa do Brasil teams: \"Flamengo - PI\" → compact full \"flamengopi\" → alias → \"flamengopi\" (Piauí club), distinct from \"flamengo\". ✓. \"Fluminense - RJ\" → \"fluminenserj\" → alias → \"fluminense\". \"Fluminense PI\" (BR-Football) → \"fluminensepi\" → \"fluminensepi\" (Piauí). ✓\n   \n   So my alias table needs entries for: {club}-ST compact forms → canonical key. This is a LOT of manual entries (725 raw names!). Alternative: build aliases PROGRAMMATICALLY at load time:\n   \n   **Programmatic disambiguation algorithm:**\n   1. Collect all raw names from all files.\n   2. For each raw name, compute compact-full (parentheses content retained, state suffix retained) and compact-base (parentheses removed, state suffix stripped).\n   3. If compact-full != compact-base (i.e., name had a state suffix or parentheses):\n      - If compact-base is \"ambiguous\" (shared by names with DIFFERENT states, or collides with a known big club), then key = compact-full (e.g. \"flamengopi\").\n      - Else key = compact-base.\n   4. If compact-full == compact-base (no suffix), key = compact-base... but \"Atletico-PR\" from Copa vs \"Atletico Paranaense\"? Hmm — wait: \"Atlético - PR\" compact-full = \"atléticopr\" → with accents folded \"atleticopr\"; and \"Athletico-PR\" (Brasileirao) → \"athleticopr\". DIFFERENT compact-fulls (atleticopr vs athleticopr) for the same club! Need manual alias to unify those two spellings. Similarly \"Atlético Paranaense - PR\" → \"atléticoparanaensepr\"→\"atleticoparanaensepr\" vs \"Athletico Paranaense\"→\"athleticoparanaense\" vs \"Atletico Paranaense\"→\"atleticoparanaense\".\n   \n   So a purely programmatic approach won't unify spelling variants (atletico/athletico, accented/unaccented). I need a HAND-CURATED alias table for the major/ambiguous clubs, plus generic fallback for the long tail of small clubs.\n\n   **Pragmatic approach:**\n   - Hand-curated ALIAS map covering: (a) all Serie A regulars 2003-2023 (~45 clubs) with all their observed spellings, (b) all FIFA Brazilian clubs (~16), (c) Libertadores Brazilian participants, (d) famous foreign clubs w/ suffix variants (Nacional/Boca/River/Peñarol... only the ones with parenthetical country codes), (e) ambiguous Copa names seen above for big clubs (the -MG -RJ -SP etc. forms of major clubs).\n   - Generic fallback: strip parens+state → compact key. For small Copa/Serie C clubs this is fine even if two \"Atlético - XX\" merge (they're tiny clubs rarely queried; and honestly \"Atletico - BA\" vs \"Atletico - AC\" — I'll add the state-suffixed aliases for atletico/america/botafogo/santacruz/flamengo/fluminense/internacional/juventude/santos/operario/river/ypiranga/rio branco/vitoria/guarani/nacional/santa rita? — I have the data, I can GENERATE the full disambiguation table programmatically at load and save manual work!\n\n   **Actually — hybrid generation**: At load time:\n   1. Every raw name → compact_full and compact_base (paren-stripped, state-stripped).\n   2. Merge compact keys that differ only by known spelling fixes: \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" pattern? NO wait — that merges Athletico-PR with Atlético-PR (SAME club, good) but the FOLDING also handles accents already. The only real spelling variants: athletico/atletico (Athletico Paranaense ↔ Atlético Paranaense ↔ Atlético-PR). Apply replacement \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" post-fold for PR club specifically? If I fold \"athleticopr\" → replace \"athletico\"→\"atletico\" → \"atleticopr\". And \"atleticoparanaense\" stays. Then \"atleticopr\" vs \"atleticoparanaense\" — still different keys for same club! Manual alias needed: atleticopr → atleticoparanaense.\n   \n   OK here's my final plan — a curated `TEAM_ALIASES` dict in normalize.py, generated with a helper script from the data (I'll produce the entries now), structured as:\n\n```python\nCANONICAL = {\n  \"flamengo\": \"Flamengo\",\n  \"fluminense\": \"Fluminense\",\n  ...\n}\nALIASES = {\n  # compact(raw) -> canonical key\n  \"flamengorj\": \"flamengo\",\n  \"fluminenserj\": \"fluminense\",\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nRules applied in order:\n1. c = compact(raw) (parens content kept, all suffixes kept)\n2. c = spelling_fixes(c) — e.g. \"athletico\"→\"atletico\"?? careful: that would fold \"athleticoparanaense\" → \"atleticoparanaense\" ✓ and \"athletico\" (bare, Libertadores 2022) → \"atletico\" ✗ collides with \"Atlético-MG\" stripped?! The bare \"Athletico\" in Libertadores must map to athleticoparanaense. Since bare \"Athletico\" compact = \"athletico\" — add explicit ALIAS \"athletico\"→\"atleticoparanaense\" BEFORE the spelling fix. Order: exact ALIASES lookup on raw compact FIRST (catches \"athletico\", \"athleticopr\", \"atleticopr\", \"atletico\", \"atleticomg\", ...), then spelling-normalize, then strip suffix, then ALIASES again, then generic.\n   \nLet me define lookup:\n```\ndef team_key(raw):\n    c = compact(raw)                        # e.g. \"Atlético Paranaense - PR\" -> \"atleticoparanaensepr\"\n    if c in ALIASES: return ALIASES[c]\n    c2 = c.replace(\"athletico\", \"atletico\") # unify spelling\n    if c2 in ALIASES: return ALIASES[c2]\n    base = compact(strip_suffix(parens_removed(raw)))  # \"atleticoparanaense\"\n    if base in ALIASES: return ALIASES[base]\n    return base or c2\n```\nWith ALIASES containing every observed suffixed/parenthesized/variant form mapped appropriately. I'll GENERATE the alias list programmatically now, review it, and hard-code it into normalize.py. 725 raw names — the generated table will be large but comprehensive. Plus display names: pick best display per key (prefer accented form, prefer most frequent raw variant, prefer shorter).\n\nHmm — wait. Generating a 725-entry table is a lot, but it can be done in code AT LOAD TIME instead of hardcoded: the loader builds the registry from the data itself + a small curated override table. That's actually the robust approach:\n\n**Runtime registry build:**\n1. Curated OVERRIDES: compact → (canonical_key, display) for ~60 major clubs and all known ambiguity fixes (atleticopr etc.).\n2. Scan all raw names; compute compact_full, compact_base.\n3. Determine base→key collisions: if two names share compact_base but resolve to different curated keys → their key stays compact_full (disambiguated by suffix). If uncurated names share a base with different states... e.g. \"Caldense - MG\" (only MG) → base \"caldense\" unique → key \"caldense\". \"Juventude - MA\" and \"Juventude - RS\": base \"juventude\" → if \"juventude\" curated → Juventude RS; then \"Juventude - MA\" must NOT map to it → key \"juventudema\" (auto). Rule: if compact_base resolves to curated key K, but the raw name's state suffix differs from K's home state → keep suffix in key. Hmm, but \"Flamengo - RJ\" has state RJ = Flamengo's home → map to \"flamengo\" ✓; \"Flamengo - PI\" state PI ≠ RJ → key \"flamengopi\" ✓. Where do I get each club's home state? From the curated table (I know the big clubs' states) + from the data itself: the most common state seen for a given base name across files! E.g. base \"america\" appears with MG (most), RN, CE... hmm \"most common\" for america: MG (América Mineiro) is most frequent in Serie A data → majority state = MG → \"america\" → americamineiro... wait but that conflicts with using compact \"america\" as the KEY for the MG club. Let me simplify: canonical key for the majority-state club = base key itself (\"america\" → display \"América Mineiro\"), minority states get suffixed keys (\"americarn\" → \"América-RN\"). For uncurated ambiguous groups same rule by majority. Edge: \"atletico\" group has GO/MG/PR/AC/BA/ES — majority across data? Atlético-MG appears most (Serie A 2003-2022) → majority = MG → \"atletico\" → Atlético Mineiro, and \"atleticogo\" → Atlético Goianiense (curated), etc. But \"Atlético - PR\" (Copa) compact_full \"atleticopr\" → curated → athleticoparanaense... wait I said unify spelling atletico/athletico: canonical key for Athletico Paranaense = \"atleticoparanaense\"? Let me set canonical key = \"atleticopr\"?? Display \"Athletico Paranaense\". Hmm — I'll choose canonical key = \"atleticoparanaense\" (folded full club name), display \"Athletico Paranaense\". Aliases: atleticopr, athleticopr, atleticoparanaensepr, athleticoparanaense, athletico, atléticopr... all → atleticoparanaense.\n   \nThis is getting complicated but it's THE core value of the whole exercise (name normalization is called out explicitly in the spec). Let me implement it as:\n\n**normalize.py structure:**\n```python\nMAJOR_CLUBS = {\n    # canonical_key: (display, home_state)\n    \"flamengo\": (\"Flamengo\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"fluminense\": (\"Fluminense\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"vascodagama\": (\"Vasco da Gama\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"botafogo\": (\"Botafogo\", \"RJ\"),\n    \"corinthians\": (\"Corinthians\", \"SP\"),\n    \"palmeiras\": (\"Palmeiras\", \"SP\"),\n    \"saopaulo\": (\"São Paulo\", \"SP\"),\n    \"santos\": (\"Santos\", \"SP\"),\n    \"gremio\": (\"Grêmio\", \"RS\"),\n    \"internacional\": (\"Internacional\", \"RS\"),\n    \"juventude\": (\"Juventude\", \"RS\"),\n    \"criciuma\": (\"Criciúma\", \"SC\"),\n    \"avai\": (\"Avaí\", \"SC\"),\n    \"chapecoense\": (\"Chapecoense\", \"SC\"),\n    \"figueirense\": (\"Figueirense\", \"SC\"),\n    \"coritiba\": (\"Coritiba\", \"PR\"),\n    \"atleticoparanaense\": (\"Athletico Paranaense\", \"PR\"),\n    \"parana\": (\"Paraná\", \"PR\"),\n    \"atleticomineiro\": (\"Atlético Mineiro\", \"MG\"),\n    \"cruzeiro\": (\"Cruzeiro\", \"MG\"),\n    \"americamineiro\": (\"América Mineiro\", \"MG\"),\n    \"bahia\": (\"Bahia\", \"BA\"),\n    \"vitoria\": (\"Vitória\", \"BA\"),\n    \"sportrecife\": (\"Sport Recife\", \"PE\"),\n    \"nautico\": (\"Náutico\", \"PE\"),\n    \"santacruz\": (\"Santa Cruz\", \"PE\"),\n    \"ceara\": (\"Ceará\", \"CE\"),\n    \"fortaleza\": (\"Fortaleza\", \"CE\"),\n    \"goias\": (\"Goiás\", \"GO\"),\n    \"atleticogoianiense\": (\"Atlético Goianiense\", \"GO\"),\n    \"vilanova\": (\"Vila Nova\", \"GO\"),\n    \"csa\": (\"CSA\", \"AL\"),\n    \"crb\": (\"CRB\", \"AL\"),\n    \"paysandu\": (\"Paysandu\", \"PA\"),\n    \"remo\": (\"Remo\", \"PA\"),\n    \"pontepreta\": (\"Ponte Preta\", \"SP\"),\n    \"portuguesa\": (\"Portuguesa\", \"SP\"),\n    \"guarani\": (\"Guarani\", \"SP\"),\n    \"saocaetano\": (\"São Caetano\", \"SP\"),\n    \"redbullbragantino\": (\"Red Bull Bragantino\", \"SP\"),\n    \"bragantino\": (\"Bragantino\", \"SP\"),  # hmm - Red Bull Bragantino was \"Bragantino\" before 2019? In novo 2019 \"RB Bragantino\"? Let me check. Keep \"bragantino\" separate alias → redbullbragantino? Historically same club. Map both.\n    \"cuiaba\": (\"Cuiabá\", \"MT\"),\n    \"joinville\": (\"Joinville\", \"SC\"),\n    ...\n}\n\nALIASES = {\n    # compact(raw) -> canonical key   (for names where generic rules fail)\n    \"atleticopr\": \"atleticoparanaense\",\n    \"athleticopr\": \"atleticoparanaense\",\n    \"athletico\": \"atleticoparanaense\",\n    \"atleticoparanaense\": \"atleticoparanaense\",  # trivially\n    \"athleticoparanaense\": \"atleticoparanaense\",\n    \"atleticoparanaensepr\": \"atleticoparanaense\",\n    \"sportclubcorinthianspaulista\": \"corinthians\",\n    \"sportclubdorecife\": \"sportrecife\",\n    \"cearasportingclub\": \"ceara\",\n    \"americafcminasgerais\": \"americamineiro\",\n    \"atleticomg\": \"atleticomineiro\",\n    \"atleticomineiromg\": \"atleticomineiro\",\n    \"atleticogo\": \"atleticogoianiense\",\n    \"atlecticogoianiense\"?: ... \n    \"vasco\": \"vascodagama\",\n    \"vascodagamarj\": \"vascodagama\",\n    \"saopaulofc\": \"saopaulo\",\n    \"saopaulosp\": \"saopaulo\",\n    \"gremiors\": \"gremio\",\n    ...\n    # Libertadores foreign clubs w/ country codes:\n    \"nacionaluru\": \"nacionaluru\",  # canonical\n    \"nacionalpar\": \"nacionalpar\",\n    \"guaranipar\": \"guaranipar\",\n    \"barcelonaecu\": \"barcelonaecu\",   # careful compact(\"Barcelona-EQU\") = \"barcelonaecu\"? fold(\"Barcelona-EQU\")=\"barcelona-equ\"→compact→\"barcelonaecu\" ✓\n    \"delfinecu\": \"delfinecu\",\n    \"riverplateuru\": \"riverplateuru\",\n    \"universitarioper\": \"universitarioper\",\n    \"trujillanosven\": \"trujillanosven\",\n    \"olimpiapar\": \"olimpiapar\",\n    \"libertadpar\": \"libertadpar\",\n}\n```\n\nWait — but for compact(\"Barcelona-EQU\"): fold lowercases everything → \"barcelonaequ\"?? No: fold(\"Barcelona-EQU\") — EQU stays \"equ\" after lowercase → compact \"barcelonaequ\". Right.\n\nThen the auto-registry: for names not matching aliases, strip parens+suffix → base compact; if base in MAJOR (canonical keys) → that; else key = base. And auto-detect collisions: if base is a MAJOR club key but the raw name's state suffix ≠ club's state → suffixed key (e.g., \"Flamengo - PI\" → base \"flamengo\" is MAJOR with state RJ ≠ PI → key \"flamengopi\"). For non-MAJOR bases with multiple states (e.g. \"Juventude - MA\" — juventude IS major (RS); MA ≠ RS → \"juventudema\" ✓; \"Operario - PR\" — operario not major; states PR/MS/MT → majority? All three distinct clubs! Auto: majority state wins base key, others get suffixed keys. Operario-PR (Operário Ferroviário, Ponta Grossa) is the most prominent (played Serie B). Hmm, majority by count in data… whatever it picks, minor clubs.)\n\nTo keep control over the important cases, MAJOR_CLUBS + ALIASES covers everything the spec's 20+ questions touch. The auto fallback handles the rest reasonably.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — do I even want the suffix-strip fallback to engage when base is a MAJOR key but state mismatches? Cases: \"Flamengo - PI\" (Copa small club Flamengo-PI de Teresina), \"Fluminense - PI\", \"Santa Cruz - RN\"/\"-RS\", \"Juventude - MA\", \"Internacional - SC\"?? wait Internacional-SC? Hmm \"Internacional - SC\" — SC? There's \"Internacional\" (RS) and... SC Internacional? Hmm — actually I think that's \"Internacional de Lages\"? Not sure. Either way: state ≠ RS → suffixed key ✓.\n\nAnd \"Botafogo - PB\"/\"Botafogo SP\" → keys \"botafogopb\", \"botafogosp\" ✓ (botafogo major has RJ). \"Santos - AP\" → \"santosap\" ✓. \"Guarani - CE\"/\"Guarani - SP\" — guarani major is SP → \"guarani\" for SP form? \"Guarani - SP\" state SP = major state → base \"guarani\" ✓; \"Guarani - CE\" → \"guaranice\" ✓; \"Guaraní (PAR)\"/\"Guaraní-PAR\" → compact_full \"guaranipar\" — in ALIASES → \"guaranipar\" ✓ (before base strip!).\n\nImportant: compact_full alias check FIRST, so \"Nacional (URU)\" → \"nacionaluru\" ✓, \"Nacional - AM\" → compact_full \"nacionalam\" → alias? If not in ALIASES, base strip → \"nacional\" — WRONG (collides with which nacional? \"nacional\" isn't a MAJOR key unless I add one... if \"nacional\" not in MAJOR and not in ALIASES → key \"nacional\" shared by Nacional-AM (Brazil) and any bare \"Nacional\". Bare \"Nacional\" doesn't exist in data (only parenthesized/suffixed). So Nacional-AM → key \"nacionalam\"?? Only if I add the state-mismatch rule for non-major bases with multiple observed states. Let me add: after computing base for uncurated names, if OTHER raw names with the same base have different stripped states → all get suffixed keys (no base claim). If all same state (or no state) → base key.\n\nSo the algorithm has a data-driven collision detection pass:\nPass 1: collect all raw names, compute compact_full + state + base.\nPass 2: for each base, collect the set of states observed. Determine claimed_state[base] = state from MAJOR if present else the single/majority state.\nPass 3: key(raw): \n  - compact_full in ALIASES → mapped key.\n  - compact_full == compact(canonical_key) of some MAJOR → that.\n  - else base: if base has a unique associated state (all raws with this base that DIDN'T alias away have same effective state, incl. None) → base; else → base+state if state else base+?? (no state but multiple states exist... e.g. bare \"Athletico\" handled by ALIASES).\n  \nUgh, complexity. Let me simplify the auto rules and accept tiny-club imperfection:\n\n**FINAL simple algorithm:**\n```\nkey(raw):\n  c = compact(raw)                       # parens kept, suffix kept\n  if c in ALIASES: return ALIASES[c]     # curated: major club variants + foreign country-coded\n  c2 = c.replace('athletico','atletico')\n  if c2 in ALIASES: return ALIASES[c2]\n  base, state = strip_suffix(remove_parens(raw))   # state = the stripped 2-3 letter code (uppercase preserved)\n  b = compact(base)\n  if b in ALIASES: return ALIASES[b]     # e.g. bare \"Vasco\" → vascodagama; \"Sao Paulo\" → saopaulo? compact(\"São Paulo\")=\"saopaulo\" — is \"saopaulo\" in ALIASES? Make ALIASES contain all canonical keys mapping to themselves, or make this a MAJOR lookup. I'll put canonical keys in ALIASES too (self-mapping) — simpler: one dict CLUBS: compact → canonical_key, where canonical keys self-map.\n  # state disambiguation:\n  if state and b is claimed by a club whose home state != state: return b + state.lower()\n  return b\n```\nWhere \"claimed club\" = CLUBS[b] exists with known home state. So:\n- \"Flamengo - PI\": c=\"flamengopi\" not in CLUBS; base \"flamengo\" in CLUBS (Flamengo, RJ); state PI ≠ RJ → key \"flamengopi\" ✓\n- \"Flamengo - RJ\": c=\"flamengorj\" in ALIASES → flamengo ✓ (or via base+state match → flamengo ✓ — I need the state-match branch: if state == home state → return canonical. If no state → return canonical (bare \"Flamengo\" → flamengo ✓).\n- \"Juventude - MA\": juventude major RS → \"juventudema\" ✓\n- \"Operario - PR\": operario NOT in CLUBS → generic: b+state? or b? If not claimed → multiple distinct clubs share... default: if state present and base unclaimed → include state: \"operariopr\", \"operarioms\", \"operariomt\" — all distinct ✓ GOOD for unclaimed bases with state.\n  But wait — then \"Palmeiras - SP\"-style: palmeiras claimed (SP) state matches → \"palmeiras\" ✓. \"Caldense - MG\" → unclaimed → \"caldensemg\" — hmm slightly ugly key but consistent. Display \"Caldense\" (base display). Fine.\n- Unclaimed + no state: base (e.g. \"Sao Paulo\" → claimed anyway ✓; \"Boca Juniors\" → \"bocajuniors\" ✓).\n- Special: names where base IS claimed but state is None: \"Grêmio\" → gremio ✓.\n\nWhat about \"America MG\" (BR-Football, space-separated state)? strip_suffix must handle \" MG\" suffix (space + 2 letters, uppercase in raw). My regex on raw: `[-–\\s]\\s*[A-Za-z]{2,3}$` — but must not strip legit words like \"Boa\" (3 letters \"Boa\"? \"Boa - MG\" → strip \"- MG\" ✓ leaves \"Boa\"). Risk: \"Sport Boys\" → \" Boys\" is 4 letters, safe. \"PSTC\" → no separator. \"URT MG\" → \" URT\"? wait \"URT MG\": strip trailing \" MG\" → \"URT\" ✓. \"CENE MS\" → \"CENE\" ✓. \"ASA AL\" → \"ASA\" ✓. \"ABC - RN\" → \"ABC\" ✓. \"CRB\"? bare ✓. \"4 de Julho EC\" → \" EC\" is 2 caps → strip → \"4 de Julho\" ✓ (fine). \"Rio Branco - Vn - ES\"?? \"Rio Branco - Vn - ES\" — weird: strip \"- ES\" → \"Rio Branco - Vn\" → \" - Vn\" not matching [A-Za-z]{2,3}$? \"Vn\" is 2 letters! → strip → \"Rio Branco\" — hmm ok whatever, tiny club.\n  BUT danger: lowercase words at end that are 2-3 letters: \"Gama - DF\" ✓ fine. What about \"Figueirense - SC\"? fine. Any club ending in a 2-3 letter WORD that's not a state? \"Boa\"! \"Boa - MG\" has separator so fine when suffixed; bare \"Boa\" (BR-Football \"Boa\") — regex needs SEPARATOR before the code, so \"Boa\" alone safe ✓. \"Mixto\", \"Trem\", \"Globo FC\" → \" FC\" caps strip → \"Globo\" ✓ (Globo FC = Globo RN; \"Globo - RN\" → \"Globo\" ✓ consistent!). \"Central SC\"?? \"Central - PE\" and \"Central SC\" — SC?? \"Central SC\" — hmm, that's probably \"Central\" (PE) with... SC suffix? Weird data. compact(\"Central SC\")... my regex strips \" SC\" (uppercase in raw) → \"central\"; \"Central - PE\" → \"central