Vitória EC\" in Copa do Brasil 2020-2022... there's \"Vitória ES\"?? Wait: group vitoria: ['Vitoria', 'Vitoria EC', 'Vitoria ES', 'Vitoria-BA', 'Vitória', 'Vitória - BA']. Hmm \"Vitoria EC\" Copa 2016-2022 — I believe this is \"Vitória-BA\"?? No wait — Vitória-BA played Copa 2021/2022 (as Serie B team, entering R4)... \"Vitoria EC\" with 3-4 matches per Copa season 2020-2022 = early rounds entrant — that's a small club: \"Vitória EC\" = Vitória de Santo Antão?? Hmm, or \"Vitória-ES\" (Espírito Santo, \"Rio Branco-Vitória\"?)... And \"Vitoria ES\" appears where? The Counter above only queried exact names in brf: \"Vitoria ES\" didn't show up in brf (empty result not printed — I queried it, no output line means zero matches? I printed all — \"Vitoria ES ->\" line missing = zero). Let me not obsess: \"Vitoria EC\" (brf, Copa) and \"Vitória - BA\"/\"Vitoria-BA\"/\"Vitória\" (Serie A/B) — if I map vitoriaec → vitoria (BA), early-round Copa rows of a small ES club would merge into big Vitória. If I keep separate: \"Vitoria EC\" Copa-only rows = separate club \"Vitória-ES\"? Reality check: Copa do Brasil 2016 first round — was there a \"Vitória-ES\" club? Hmm, \"Rio Branco-ES\"... Actually there IS \"Vitória Futebol Clube\"?? no... You know what — I'll map \"vitoriaec\" → vitoria (BA). Rationale: in BR-Football, Serie A 2021-2023 Vitória-BA rows use bare \"Vitoria\" — and Copa \"Vitoria EC\" 2021 3 matches — Vitória-BA DID play Copa 2021 (entered round of 16 as Serie B 2020 champion? no...). Hmm, 2021 Copa: Vitória-BA entered in the round of 32 (third stage)! \"Copa do Brasil 2021: {3: 32, 4: 16}\" — Vitória-BA played R3 2 legs + ? \"Vitoria EC\" Copa 2021 = 3 matches. Vitória played R3 (2 legs vs ???) hmm 3 matches though... \n\n   Ugh, I'm spending too long on a tiny club. DECISION: map \"vitoriaec\"→vitoria. If slightly wrong on a few Copa rows, acceptable for demo purposes. Similarly \"fortalezafc\"→fortaleza, \"botafogosp\" stays separate (it's clearly Botafogo-SP Ribeirão Preto, Serie B/C regular — keep as own club \"Botafogo-SP\"), \"americarn\" own club \"América-RN\" ✓, \"nacionalam\"?? didn't appear in brf (empty) — it's from the team-name groups earlier (Libertadores? no — 'Nacional - AM'... hmm it appeared in the grouping so it's in SOME file — let me not worry; generic rules handle it: base \"nacional\" unclaimed + state am → \"nacionalam\" ✓).\n   \n   Wait — one more: \"Atletico - PR\" and \"Athletico\" queries returned EMPTY in brf — so where do 'Atletico - PR', 'Athletico', 'Internacional - SC' come from? They're in the name groups... from COPA file (\"Atletico - PR\"?) and LIB file (\"Athletico\", \"Internacional - SC\"?? hmm lib has \"Internacional\"? Yes lib has \"Internacional\" bare. \"Internacional - SC\"... maybe from copa). Fine — generic + alias rules cover.\n\n4. **bra vs novo date agreement**: only 100/380 same-day dates → the two files disagree on dates a lot (one is probably correct kickoff, other maybe scheduled date). Doesn't matter — we pick one source.\n\n5. **Libertadores finals**: 2 legs 2013-2018, 1 leg 2019, 2020 (\"NA\" row = the 2022 final second leg with no data — so 2021, 2022 finals missing?! lib finals/season: 2013:2...2019:1, 2020:1, NA:1 — 2021 MISSING, 2022 = the NA row only?? Hmm: 2021 final (Palmeiras-Flamengo, single leg Nov 2021) — count shows no 2021 final! And 2022 final (Flamengo-Athletico) = the NA row (no score). So the lib file has 2013-2020 finals + broken 2022. The 2021 final missing entirely. Data reality. Fine — finals query returns what exists. (Also note lib 2021 season = 124 rows vs real ~126? close.)\n\nOK. Data understood. Now let me check the mcp 2.1.1 API surface to write the server + integration tests correctly, and install pytest-bdd + pytest-asyncio.\n\nThen write the code:\n\n**models.py**: \n```python\n@dataclass Match: id, date, competition_id, competition, season, stage, round (int|None), home_key, home_team, away_key, away_team, home_goals, away_goals, stadium, kickoff (time str), stats: MatchStats|None (corners, shots, attacks, ht_result), source\n@dataclass Player: id, name, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, club_key, position, jersey, height, weight, preferred_foot, value, wage, skills: dict\n@dataclass TeamStat: matches, wins, draws, losses, gf, ga ...\n```\n\n**loader.py**:\n- parse helpers: parse_int (None on NA/-), parse_float, parse_date (formats: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\")\n- load_players(fifa)\n- load raw matches per file → list of RawMatch (home_raw, away_raw, ...)\n- build competition groups: brasileirao [bra, novo, brf-SerieA], copadobrasil [cup, brf-Copa], libertadores [lib], serieb [brf-SerieB], seriec [brf-SerieC]\n- for brf rows: season = year - 1 if month <= 3 else year (for all tournaments — applies to Serie A/B/C/Copa)\n- per season: pick source (max scored, tie → preference order); parse rows → Match objects (skip unparseable: no date or no goals; count skipped)\n- dedup exact (date, home_key, away_key, home_goals, away_goals) within chosen source\n- enrich: brf stats map (date, home_key, away_key) → stats; novo arena map (date, home, away) → stadium; apply to matches from other sources when joinable\n- build SoccerData with indexes: matches (list), by_team: dict key→list indices, by_competition, players, players_by_club, players_by_name tokens?, team_registry (all observed variants, display names), competitions meta (seasons, sources per season)\n- Also a load_stats dict: rows per file, skipped counts, chosen source per competition-season (for transparency / team_competitions?)\n\n**analysis.py** functions returning structured result objects (or dicts) that server.py formats:\nActually simpler: analysis functions return formatted strings directly? For testability, return data structures; formatting helpers in same module produce strings; MCP tools call analysis + format. I'll have analysis return dataclasses/dicts, and format_* functions render the spec-style text. Tests assert on data + formatted output strings.\n\nFunctions:\n- list_competitions() → [{id, display, seasons, match_count, team_count, sources}]\n- resolve_team(q) → TeamInfo (key, display, variants, states) — raises/None if not found\n- search_matches(team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20) → {matches: [Match], total, truncated, team_resolved, ...}\n- head_to_head(a, b, competition=None) → matches + record {a_wins, b_wins, draws} + per-competition breakdown\n- last_match_between(a, b)\n- team_stats(team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None) → TeamStat + breakdown; team_profile: competitions, seasons, totals\n- team_competitions(team) → per-competition counts\n- search_players(name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, sort='overall', limit) → [Player]\n- player_details(name) → [Player full]\n- standings(competition, season, include_position) → rows sorted by pts/gd/gf; champion; relegated (bottom 4) for brasileirao; only league comps (brasileirao, serieb, seriec)\n- champion(competition, season) → league: standings[0]; cup: winner of final (aggregate over legs? final matches: winner by aggregate, penalties not in data — if two legs sum; if tie on aggregate... data doesn't have shootout info; report both / \"decided on penalties (not in data)\"). For libertadores/copa finals: sum goals across final legs.\n- competition_finals(competition, season=None) → final matches per season\n- biggest_wins(competition=None, season=None, limit=10) → sorted by margin desc\n- competition_stats(competition=None, season=None) → avg goals/match, home/away/draw rates\n- home_away_split → home wins vs away wins rates, avg home goals vs away goals\n- best_records(venue, competition=None, season=None, min_matches=10, limit=10) → ranked teams by win rate (then points rate)\n- derbies(season=None, competition=None) → known derby matches + labels\n- compare_seasons(sa, sb, competition='brasileirao') → side-by-side totals\n- search_teams(q) → fuzzy matches list\n\n**server.py**: FastMCP(\"brazilian-soccer\"), tools wrapping analysis with formatted output; module-level `_DATA = load_soccer_data(path)` lazily (on first tool call — to keep import fast for tests? No — load at startup; stdio server startup < 1-2s fine. Actually FIFA 18k rows + 24k matches parse fast (<1s). Load eagerly at module import in main; for tests, use lazy function param). I'll structure server.py: `def build_server(data=None) -> FastMCP` so tests can build with fixture data; `python server.py` runs with real data. \n\n**Knowledge-graph aspect**: The spec says \"knowledge graph interface\" — I'll frame it in README: entities (teams, players, competitions, matches) + relations (played, hosts, member_of_club, head_to_head) exposed via MCP tools. team_profile will list related entities. Good enough.\n\n**Tests** (pytest-bdd Gherkin, GWT):\n- features/match_queries.feature — spec scenarios (Flamengo-Fluminense, Palmeiras 2023, Copa finals, last match, date-range search)\n- features/team_queries.feature — Corinthians home 2022, top scorers 2023 team goals, Palmeiras-Santos h2h\n- features/player_queries.feature — Brazilian players, top-rated at club, forwards from São Paulo FC, Gabriel Barbosa lookup, filter nationality+club\n- features/competition_queries.feature — 2019 champion, standings 2018, relegated 2020, competitions of Palmeiras\n- features/statistics.feature — avg goals, best away record, biggest wins, home advantage, compare seasons\n- features/normalization.feature — name variants map to same team (Palmeiras-SP vs Palmeiras vs full names, dates formats)\n- features/mcp_server.feature — server lists tools, responds to queries (integration over stdio or in-memory session)\n- features/performance.feature — response time assertions (<2s simple, <5s aggregate)\n- conftest.py: fixtures — loaded SoccerData (session-scoped), MCP client (session-scoped, in-memory via anyio memory streams or stdio subprocess)\n\npytest-bdd: install. For async MCP client tests: use `mcp.client.session.ClientSession` with `mcp.shared.memory.create_client_server_stream`? In mcp python SDK there's `create_connected_server_and_client` helper in tests... simpler: stdio_client(StdioServerParameters(command=sys.executable, args=[\"server.py\"])) — real subprocess test, marked as integration. Needs anyio pytest plugin (anyio itself provides pytest plugin via `pytest.ini` anyio_mode? Actually `anyio.pytest_plugin` — plugin ships with anyio, enable via `pytest_plugins = ['anyio']`... hmm, the mcp stdio_client is an async context manager. pytest-asyncio simpler: mark tests `@pytest.mark.asyncio`. Install pytest-asyncio too.\n\nFor BDD steps that need async, pytest-bdd + pytest-asyncio don't compose directly (async step functions aren't supported by pytest-bdd until... pytest-bdd supports async steps? pytest-bdd 7+ has no native async step support. Workaround: in the step (sync), use `anyio.run(...)` or `asyncio.run(...)` inside the step definition. That works fine.)\n\nTiming tests: performance assertions — measure query wall time.\n\nLet me now also confirm mcp FastMCP tool decorator usage in 2.1.1 (mcp-types 2.1.1?? The package installed is \"mcp-2.1.1\" — quite new. Hmm, versions: mcp python SDK went 1.x → 2.x recently (2025). FastMCP still at mcp.server.fastmcp? Let me verify import paths and stdio client quickly with a smoke script.\n\nAlso install pytest-bdd & pytest-asyncio now.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c4f751c001O1VY130f5DGUNp","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c4e12bd001rqvnGIDrV3Itar"}}
