 a tool.\n\nTools can optionally request a Context object by adding a parameter with the\nContext type annotation. The context provides access to MCP capabilities like\nlogging, progress reporting, and resource access.\n\nArgs:\n    name: Optional name for the tool (defaults to function name)\n    title: Optional human-readable title for the tool\n    description: Optional description of what the tool does\n    annotations: Optional ToolAnnotations providing additional tool information\n    icons: Optional list of icons for the tool\n    meta: Optional metadata dictionary for the tool\nclient: ['CacheConfig', 'CacheEntry', 'CacheKey', 'CacheMode', 'ClaimContext', 'Client', 'ClientExtension', 'ClientRequestContext', 'ClientSession', 'InMemoryResponseCacheStore', 'IncomingMessage', 'InputRequiredRoundsExceededError', 'NotificationBinding', 'ResponseCacheStore', 'ResultClaim', 'Transport', 'UnexpectedClaimedResult', 'advertise', 'caching', 'client', 'context', 'extension', 'session', 'session_group', 'sse', 'stdio', 'streamable_http', 'subscriptions']\nstdio_client ok\n","metadata":{"output":"run sig: (self, transport: \"Literal['stdio', 'sse', 'streamable-http']\" = 'stdio', **kwargs: 'Any') -> 'None'\nrun doc: Run the MCP server. Note this is a synchronous function.\n\nArgs:\n    transport: Transport protocol to use (\"stdio\", \"sse\", or \"streamable-http\")\n    **kwargs: Transport-specific options (see overloads for details)\n\ncall_tool sig: (self, name: 'str', arguments: 'dict[str, Any]', context: 'Context[LifespanResultT, Any] | None' = None) -> 'CallToolResult | InputRequiredResult'\ncall_tool doc: Call a tool by name with arguments.\n\nRaises:\n    ToolError: If the tool is unknown, the arguments fail validation, or the\n        tool (or a resolver) raises `ToolError` or `ResourceError`.\n    UnexpectedToolError: If the tool (or a resolver) raises anything else, or\n        its return value fails output conversion. `__cause__` is the original\n        exception (or, for a nested tool or resource crash, its wrapper).\n    MCPError: Raised by the tool or a resolver; passed through unchanged.\n\ntool doc: Decorator to register a tool.\n\nTools can optionally request a Context object by adding a parameter with the\nContext type annotation. The context provides access to MCP capabilities like\nlogging, progress reporting, and resource access.\n\nArgs:\n    name: Optional name for the tool (defaults to function name)\n    title: Optional human-readable title for the tool\n    description: Optional description of what the tool does\n    annotations: Optional ToolAnnotations providing additional tool information\n    icons: Optional list of icons for the tool\n    meta: Optional metadata dictionary for the tool\nclient: ['CacheConfig', 'CacheEntry', 'CacheKey', 'CacheMode', 'ClaimContext', 'Client', 'ClientExtension', 'ClientRequestContext', 'ClientSession', 'InMemoryResponseCacheStore', 'IncomingMessage', 'InputRequiredRoundsExceededError', 'NotificationBinding', 'ResponseCacheStore', 'ResultClaim', 'Transport', 'UnexpectedClaimedResult', 'advertise', 'caching', 'client', 'context', 'extension', 'session', 'session_group', 'sse', 'stdio', 'streamable_http', 'subscriptions']\nstdio_client ok\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"source venv/bin/activate && python - <<'EOF'\nimport inspect\nfrom mcp.server.mcpserver import MCPServer\nprint(\"run sig:\", inspect.signature(MCPServer.run))\nprint(\"run doc:\", (inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.run) or \"\")[:500])\nprint()\nprint(\"call_tool sig:\", inspect.signature(MCPServer.call_tool))\nprint(\"call_tool doc:\", (inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.call_tool) or \"\")[:800])\nprint()\nprint(\"tool doc:\", (inspect.getdoc(MCPServer.tool) or \"\")[:600])\nimport mcp.client as c\nprint(\"client:\", [n for n in dir(c) if not n.startswith('_')])\nfrom mcp.client.stdio import stdio_client\nprint(\"stdio_client ok\")\nEOF","time":{"start":1788255140226,"end":1788255140258}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good — mcp 2.x MCPServer has:\n- `.tool()` decorator\n- `.run()` (probably detects transport?), `.run_stdio_async()`, `.run_sse_async()`, `.run_streamable_http_async()`\n- `.call_tool()`, `.list_tools()` async methods — great for in-memory testing!\n\nAnd the client side. Let me check `mcp.client` for ClientSession and stdio_client and whether there's an in-memory helper. Also check how `run()` works — signature and what transport arg it takes.\n\nActually — decision point: use mcp 2.x (installed, current) vs pin mcp 1.x. The MCP ecosystem widely documents FastMCP 1.x style, but 2.x is what's installed and current. Using 2.x is better here (no downgrade needed). The API: MCPServer + tool decorator + run_stdio_async. For testing: I can call `server.call_tool(...)` directly in-memory! That's excellent for BDD tests without subprocess complexity. Plus one true stdio integration test using the client.\n\nLet me check the client API and `run` signature, then check how call_tool works (name, arguments). Also check what a Tool result looks like (structured content?).\n\nLet me explore mcp.client and try a quick end-to-end smoke test in-memory with a temp server.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c4ff01e001GQD29KFc1aqn8U","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c4fd437001mZnhbzZaca8NR4"}}
