tch of the final stage? We don't have stage info in brf. The last match overall of the season: winner (or if draw... finals in Serie C are 2 legs). Hmm — skip champion for seriec; document limitation. Actually — I CAN do \"last match between the two most-frequent-teams-late-in-season\"... overkill. Series C champion: report \"not supported for Serie C (knockout format)\" or compute from the latest matches: find the two teams playing on the last matchday with max date, aggregate... I'll simply try: take the last N matches at the max date; the champion = winner by aggregate of the pairing that played on the season's final date. If ambiguous, say so. Actually let me just do: champion for league comps = standings[0] (brasileirao, serieb only); cup comps (copadobrasil, libertadores) = final winner; seriec = \"not determinable reliably\" message. Fine — pragmatic.\n\nFor dup pairing handling in standings: group by pairing, keep latest date. For match search: keep all rows (dedup only exact same (date,teams,goals)).\n\nNOW let me build. Directory plan:\n\n```\nserver.py\nbrazilian_soccer/\n  __init__.py\n  normalize.py\n  models.py\n  loader.py\n  analysis.py\n  formatting.py\ntests/\n  __init__.py (empty, not needed with pytest rootdir config)\n  conftest.py\n  features/\n    match_queries.feature\n    team_queries.feature\n    player_queries.feature\n    competition_queries.feature\n    statistics.feature\n    normalization.feature\n    mcp_server.feature\n    performance.feature\n  test_match_queries.py\n  test_team_queries.py\n  test_player_queries.py\n  test_competition_queries.py\n  test_statistics.py\n  test_normalization.py\n  test_mcp_server.py\n  test_performance.py\npytest.ini\nrequirements.txt\nREADME.md (update)\n```\n\nLet me carefully write each module now. Start with normalize.py — the heart of team name handling.\n\n**normalize.py** contents:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Team-name normalization ...\"\"\"\nimport re\nimport unicodedata\n\ndef fold(text) -> str: NFKD strip combining, lower, collapse whitespace\ndef compact(text) -> str: fold + keep alnum only\n\nSTATE_SUFFIX_RE = re.compile(r'[\\s\\-–]+\\s*([A-Za-z]{2,3})$')\n\n# canonical major clubs: key -> (display, state)\nMAJOR_CLUBS = {...}\n# compact(raw variant) -> canonical key\nALIASES = {...}\n# canonical keys self-map\n\ndef _strip_state(raw): \n    # remove parenthetical content, then trailing 2-3 letter code\n    no_parens = re.sub(r'\\([^)]*\\)', ' ', raw).strip()  # used for base\n    m = STATE_SUFFIX_RE.search(no_parens)... \n```\n\nCareful with the state-suffix regex on raw (non-folded) text: the code must be UPPERCASE in the raw name to strip (\"Boa\" must not strip; \"America MG\" MG is uppercase ✓; \"Sao Jose - POA\" POA uppercase ✓; \"Sao Jose PA\" PA uppercase ✓). Regex: r'(?:[-–]|\\s)\\s*([A-Z]{2,3})$' — on the paren-stripped string. Cases: \"Palmeiras-SP\" ✓; \"América - MG\" ✓; \"America MG\" ✓ (space); \"Barcelona-EQU\" ✓; \"Rio Branco - Vn - ES\": strip \" - ES\" → \"Rio Branco - Vn\" (stops — \"Vn\" is Mixed case, no second strip). Good.\nEdge: \"CSA\" alone — no separator → safe. \"LDU\" safe. \"URT MG\" → strips \" MG\" ✓. \"4 de Julho EC\" → strips \" EC\"?? \"EC\" is uppercase 2 letters → stripped → \"4 de Julho\". Hmm — that merges \"4 de Julho EC\" with hypothetical bare \"4 de Julho\" — they're the same club (Piauí). Fine.\nEdge risk: legit club names ending in an uppercase 2-3 letter token that is NOT a state/country code: \"Boa\" (not uppercase B-o-a? \"Boa\" — mixed case → regex [A-Z]{2,3} requires ALL caps → \"Boa\" fails ✓ safe). \"Mixto\"? not caps. \"PSTC\" — no separator... \"Trem AP\" — AP is a state ✓ correct strip. OK — but what about FIFA club \"CRB\"? bare, safe. What about \"G.O. (Espanha)\"?? not present. OK.\n\nHmm — one more: the suffix strip should also try repeatedly? \"Rio Branco - Vn - ES\" leaves \"- Vn\" junk. Rare; ignore.\n\nAlso \"Santos FC\" (FIFA)? Is there \"Santos FC\"? FIFA club list had 'Santos' ✓ bare. And 'Santos Laguna' separate ✓.\n\n**team_key(raw)**:\n```python\ndef team_key(raw: str) -> str:\n    name = (raw or \"\").strip()\n    if not name: return \"\"\n    c = compact(name)  # parens content KEPT, suffix KEPT\n    if c in ALIASES:\n        return ALIASES[c]\n    # strip parenthetical content and trailing state/country code\n    base, state = split_base(name)\n    b = compact(base)\n    if b in ALIASES:\n        key = ALIASES[b]\n        club = MAJOR_CLUBS.get(key)\n        if club and state and state.upper() != club.state:\n            return b + state.lower()   # different club sharing the name\n        if club and not state:\n            return key\n        if club and state and state.upper() == club.state:\n            return key\n    if state:\n        return b + state.lower()   # unclaimed base with state → state-suffixed key\n    return b or c\n```\nHmm wait, there's an inconsistency: when b in ALIASES → key resolved; when state mismatch → return \"b+state\" — but then variants \"Flamengo - PI\" (key \"flamengopi\") and BR-Football \"Flamengo PI\"?? — does \"Flamengo PI\" exist? Group flamengo: ['Flamengo', 'Flamengo - PI', 'Flamengo - RJ', 'Flamengo-RJ'] — only paren/suffix variants; brf uses \"Flamengo - PI\"?? The \" - PI\" form (with spaces) is the copa/brf style. Both \"Flamengo - PI\" and \"Flamengo-RJ\"-style compact to \"flamengopi\"/\"flamengorj\" — different separator styles compact identically ✓. So \"flamengopi\" merges across styles ✓.\n\nWhen base NOT in ALIASES and state exists → \"b+state\" — e.g. \"Operario - PR\" → \"operariopr\" ✓; and BR-Football \"Operario PR\" → same ✓; and \"Operario-PR\"?? → same ✓. \n\nWhen base not in ALIASES and NO state → b. E.g. \"Boca Juniors\" → \"bocajuniors\" ✓. \"Sao Paulo\" → b = \"saopaulo\" → in ALIASES (self-canonical) ✓.\n\nBUT the unclaimed+state case produces keys like \"caldensemg\" while a hypothetical bare \"Caldense\" → \"caldense\" — split identity. Acceptable.\n\nAlso — MAJOR_CLUBS vs ALIASES redundancy: I'll define `CLUBS: dict[key] = Club(display, state)` and `ALIASES: dict[compact_variant] = key` where canonical keys also appear in ALIASES self-mapped. team_key checks ALIASES first (full compact), then base path.\n\nThe **display name** for computed keys (e.g. \"operariopr\"): prettify — title-case the base + \" (\" + state + \")\"? I'll build a TeamRegistry at load: for every raw name encountered, compute key; registry[key] accumulates variants; display = MAJOR display if key in CLUBS else pick the most common raw variant, paren/suffix-stripped prettified. E.g. key \"operariopr\" display \"Operário\" (from raw \"Operário - PR\" stripped)? Multiple raw spellings (\"Operario - PR\", \"Operário - PR\") — pick most frequent; ties → longest (accented) form? I'll pick most frequent, tie-break by accented-ness then length. Fine.\n\n**MAJOR_CLUBS table** (canonical keys with display+state):\n\nflamengo RJ, fluminense RJ, botafogo RJ, vascodagama RJ, corinthians SP, palmeiras SP, saopaulo SP, santos SP, pontepreta SP, portuguesa SP, guarani SP, saocaetano SP, redbullbragantino SP, bragantino→alias, gremio RS, internacional RS, juventude RS, coritiba PR, atleticoparanaense PR, parana PR, atleticomineiro MG, cruzeiro MG, americamineiro MG, bahia BA, vitoria BA, sportrecife PE, nautico PE, santacruz PE, ceara CE, fortaleza CE, goias GO, atleticogoianiense GO, vilanova GO, crb AL, csa AL, paysandu PA, remo PA, avai SC, chapecoense SC, criciuma SC, figueirense SC, joinville SC, cuiaba MT, brasiliense DF, ipatinga MG, santoandre SP, barueri SP, santacruz... also guaranydesobral? no — small. Grêmio Prudente (2010 Serie A!) — \"Grêmio Prudente\" is in novo — NOT major table but appears in Serie A 2010 → generic key \"gremioprudente\" ✓ fine.\n\n**ALIASES** — comprehensive list. Let me now generate it from data + my curation. I'll write a helper script to produce candidate entries, then embed. Actually — simpler: I'll hand-write the table carefully based on the observed groups (I have the full group dump above). Let me enumerate alias entries (compact → key):\n\nSelf-canonical entries (so base-lookup works): each canonical key maps to itself — implemented by checking CLUBS in the base path (no need for ALIASES self entries; base path: if b in CLUBS → state check → return key). So ALIASES only needs NON-canonical compact forms. Let me list (from observed variants):\n\nflamengo group: flamengorj→flamengo (also \"Flamengo - RJ\" ✓). \"flamengopi\" → NOT aliased → base path: b=\"flamengo\" in CLUBS, state PI≠RJ → \"flamengopi\" ✓.\nfluminense: fluminenserj→fluminense. (\"Fluminense - RJ\" ✓; \"Fluminense RJ\" ✓)\nbotafogo: botafogorj→botafogo; botafogosp → base path b=\"botafogo\" state SP≠RJ → \"botafogosp\" ✓ auto; botafogopb → \"botafogopb\" auto ✓. No aliases needed for those! Only botafogorj needed (state matches anyway → base path returns \"botafogo\" — wait: b=\"botafogo\" in CLUBS, state RJ == RJ → key \"botafogo\" ✓ auto!). So even botafogorj unneeded! The state-match logic handles suffixed forms of major clubs automatically. \nvascodagama: \"Vasco da Gama - RJ\" → base \"Vasco da Gama\" → b=\"vascodagama\" in CLUBS, state RJ match → \"vascodagama\" ✓ auto. \"Vasco Da Gama RJ\" ✓ auto. \"Vasco\" (bare) → b=\"vasco\" not in CLUBS → hmm! Need alias vasco→vascodagama ✓ REQUIRED.\ncorinthians: \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" (FIFA) → b=\"sportclubcorinthianspaulista\" → ALIAS REQUIRED → corinthians. suffixed auto ✓.\npalmeiras: auto ✓ (no odd variants).\nsaopaulo: \"Sao Paulo FC\"? not observed. \"São Paulo - SP\" auto ✓. Hmm — FIFA has no São Paulo. OK no aliases.\nsantos: auto (suffixed SP form auto; \"Santos - AP\" → santosap auto ✓).\npontepreta: \"Ponte Preta-SP\" auto ✓.\nportuguesa: auto ✓. \"Portuguesa RJ\" (brf? \"Portuguesa RJ\" observed!) → b=portuguesa state RJ vs SP mismatch → \"portuguesarj\" — hmm \"Portuguesa RJ\"?? Observed variants: ['Portuguesa', 'Portuguesa - SP', 'Portuguesa RJ', 'Portuguesa-SP'] — \"Po