 suffix → base \"Atlético\" b=\"atletico\" NOT in CLUBS → state MG → return \"atleticomg\"!! SAME KEY. So the alias is redundant BUT the key \"atleticomg\" ≠ canonical \"atleticomineiro\" ✗. So ALIAS atleticomg→atleticomineiro REQUIRED ✓ (as planned). Similarly atleticogo→atleticogoianiense ✓ REQUIRED.\n\n\"Americamg\" → compact_full → ALIAS → americamineiro ✓ REQUIRED (else base \"America\"/\"América\" unclaimed → \"americamg\" ≠ canonical ✗).\n\"americarn\" — América-RN: canonical club? Add to CLUBS: americarn (\"América-RN\", \"RN\") — then \"América - RN\" compact_full \"americarn\" → ALIAS self ✓ or base path: b=\"america\" not claimed → state RN → \"americarn\" ✓ auto. Just add CLUB entry for display; no alias needed. Also \"americace\"/\"americasc\" if observed — not observed; skip.\n\nAlso — \"americamineiro\" canonical — suffixed form \"América - MG\" → ALIAS ✓ done.\n\nHmm what about \"America\" bare (brf \"America\"?? — is there a bare \"America\" in brf? group: ['America - MG', 'America MG', 'America RN', 'America-MG', 'América - MG', 'América - RN', 'América-MG', 'América-RN'] — no bare. ✓. But wait — \"America MG\"/\"America RN\" (brf space form) → compact_full \"americamg\"/\"americarn\" → ALIAS ✓ / auto ✓.\n\nMore: \"saobento\"? \"São Bento - SP\" auto → \"saobento\" unclaimed ✓ fine.\n\n\"tuntum EC\" — 'tuntum EC' (lowercase t!) observed in brf. Auto → base \"tuntum\"?? \"tuntum EC\" strip \" EC\" → \"tuntum\" ✓ fine.\n\n\"GE Gloria\" ✓ auto.\n\nOK — and FIFA \"América de Cali\" → compact \"americadecali\" → not claimed → key ✓ distinct. lib \"América de Cali\" same ✓ merge across files ✓ correct (same club).\n\nOne more critical thing: brf Serie A 2023 team names — let me enumerate them to ensure they all resolve correctly through my rules (aliases/state-matching). I'll do that in a verification script after writing normalize.py — run ALL team names from all files through team_key and eyeball the key map + check no major club splits. Also programmatically check: for the big-12 clubs, all their variants land on one key.\n\n**FIFA club → key for player queries**: same team_key() on Club. \"FC Barcelona\"→\"fcbarcelona\" fine. Brazilian clubs resolve to canonical keys ✓ → cross-file queries work (e.g., players at \"Grêmio\" → club_key \"gremio\" ↔ match teams \"Grêmio\" ✓).\n\n**Now models.py**:\n\n```python\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass MatchStats: corners_home, corners_away, shots_home, shots_away, attacks_home, attacks_away, ht_result_home, ht_result_away (str WON/LOST/DRAW), \n@dataclass\nclass Match:\n    match_id: str\n    competition_id: str\n    competition: str\n    season: int|None\n    date: date|None\n    kickoff: str|None\n    stage: str|None        # \"Round 22\", \"Final\", \"group stage\", \"Semifinal\"...\n    round_number: int|None\n    home_key, home_team, away_key, away_team: str\n    home_goals, away_goals: int\n    stadium: str|None\n    stats: MatchStats|None\n    source: str             # file display name\n    def winner(self) -> str|None: 'home'/'away'/'draw'\n    def loser..., margin etc.\n@dataclass\nclass Player:\n    player_id, name, name_normalized, age, nationality, overall, potential, club, club_key, position, jersey, height, weight, preferred_foot, value, wage, skills (dict[str,int])\n@dataclass\nclass TeamRecord: matches, wins, draws, losses, goals_for, goals_against → points, win_rate props\n@dataclass\nclass StandingRow: position, team_key, team, played, wins, draws, losses, goals_for, goals_against, goal_diff, points\n@dataclass\nclass TeamInfo: key, display, states, variants, sources\n@dataclass\nclass CompetitionInfo: id_, display, seasons (list[int]), match_count, team_count, description\n```\n\n**loader.py**:\n\n```python\nDATA_FILES = {\n  \"brasileirao_matches\": \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\",\n  \"brazilian_cup\": \"Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\",\n  \"libertadores\": \"Libertadores_Matches.csv\",\n  \"br_football\": \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\",\n  \"novo\": \"novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\",\n  \"fifa\": \"fifa_data.csv\",\n}\nparse_date(s): try formats [\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\", \"%Y-%m-%d\", \"%d/%m/%Y\"]; return None on fail\nparse_int(s): int(s) if digits else None\nparse_float similarly\n\nclass SourceFile parsing funcs returning list[RawMatch(date, season, home_raw, away_raw, hg, ag, round_no, stage, stadium, kickoff, stats)]\n\nCompetition defs:\nCOMPETITIONS = [\n  (\"brasileirao\", \"Brasileirão\", [ (bra_file_parser, key \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\"), (novo_parser), (brf_SerieA_parser) ]),\n  (\"copadobrasil\", \"Copa do Brasil\", [cup, brf_Copa]),\n  (\"libertadores\", \"Copa Libertadores\", [lib]),\n  (\"serieb\", \"Brasileirão Serie B\", [brf_SerieB]),\n  (\"seriec\", \"Brasileirão Serie C\", [brf_SerieC]),\n]\n\nbuild_competition_matches(raw_by_source, preference):\n  seasons = all seasons (None season → try infer from date year? lib NA row has no date → dropped)\n  for season: \n     scored_counts per source\n     best = max count\n     chosen = first in preference order with count >= 0.8*best and count>0 else argmax\n     rows = parse; for each row: team keys; skip if no date or goals None\n     dedup exact (date, home_key, away_key, hg, ag)\n     build Match objects (stage formatting: leagues → f\"Round {round}\"; copa → f\"Round {round}\" with label max-round→\"Final\"; lib → stage as-is title-cased)\n  stats maps from brf (all tournaments) keyed (date, home_key, away_key) → MatchStats — but note: stats join must happen for the CHOSEN matches including when brf itself is the source (stats are native then: keep in-row stats). For non-brf chosen rows, look up join map.\n  arena map from novo keyed (date, home_key, away_key).\n  \nMatch id: f\"{competition_id}-{season}-{idx:04d}\" or hash — simple sequential per competition+season.\n```\n\nHmm — the brf stats map keys use brf's OWN dates; chosen bra rows have bra dates; join works only when dates agree (rare-ish). Also, when brf IS the chosen source, stats come inline. For simplicity: build `stats_join: dict[(date,h,a)] -> MatchStats` from ALL brf rows; when constructing a Match from a non-brf source, look up. When from brf, use inline (same thing).\n\n`SoccerData` class fields:\n- matches: list[Match]\n- by_competition: dict[comp_id → list[Match]] (sorted by date)\n- by_team: dict[team_key → list[Match]] (sorted by date)\n- players: list[Player]; players_by_club: dict[club_key → list]; \n- team_registry: dict[key → TeamInfo]\n- competitions: dict[id → CompetitionInfo]\n- source_selection: dict[(comp_id, season) → (source_name, per-source counts)] for transparency\n- load_report: counts per file, skipped rows, etc.\n\n`load_soccer_data(data_dir=\"data/kaggle\") -> SoccerData` — main entry. ~37k matches total? Let me estimate: brasileirao 2003-2023 ≈ 552+552+462+380*17 (2006-2022) + 377 ≈ 8755? plus copa ~1200, lib 1249, serieb ~3400, seriec ~1900 → ~16.5k matches. Players 18k. Load time: pure Python parsing ~1-2s. Acceptable (server startup) — and < 2s query requirement unaffected (load once).\n\n**analysis.py** — functions (all take SoccerData, return result objects):\n\n```python\nclass AnalysisError(ValueError) — for unknown team/competition etc. → MCP tool returns friendly message instead of error.\n\nresolve_team(data, query) → TeamInfo (exact key match; else substring; else difflib best; raise AnalysisError listing close options)\nresolve_competition(data, query) → comp id\nsearch_matches(data, team=None, opponent=None, competition=None, season=None, date_from=None, date_to=None, limit=20) → SearchResult(matches truncated list, total, team_info, opponent_info, competition_info)\nhead_to_head(data, team_a, team_b, competition=None) → H2HResult(matches, record dict, per_comp)\nlast_match_between(data, a, b) → Match|None\nteam_stats(data, team, season=None, competition=None, venue=None) → TeamStatsResult(team, season, competition, venue, overall TeamRecord, home rec, away rec, matches list?)\nteam_profile(data, team) → per-competition records, seasons, totals\nteam_competitions(data, team) → [{competition, seasons, matches, record}]\nsearch_players(data, name=None, nationality=None, club=None, position=None, min_overall=None, max_overall=None, limit=20, sort_by=\"overall\") → [Player] (+ resolved club info)\nplayer_details(data, name) → [Player] full\nstandings(data, competition, season) → StandingsResult(rows, champion, relegated, note)\nchampion(data, competition, season) → (team display, how determined, detail)\ncompetition_finals(data, competition, season=None) → list of (season, [Match], winner?) \nbiggest_wins(data, competition=None, season=None, limit=10) → [Match] sorted by margin\ncompetition_stats(data, competition=None, season=None) → {matches, goals, avg, home_wins, draws, away_wins, home_win_pct, avg_home_goals, avg_away_goals}\nbest_records(data, venue, competition=None, season=None, min_matches=10, limit=10) → [(TeamInfo, TeamRecord, win_rate)]\nderbies(data, season=None, competition=None) → [(label, Match)]\ncompare_seasons(data, season_a, season_b, competition=\"brasileirao\") → side by side dicts + champion of each\nsearch_teams(data, query) → [TeamInfo] fuzzy\nlist_competitions(data) → [CompetitionInfo]\n```\n\nDERBIES table:\n```python\nDERBIES = [\n  (\"Fla-Flu\", \"flamengo\", \"fluminense\"),\n  (\"Clássico dos Milhões\", \"flamengo\", \"vascodagama\"),\n  (\"Clássico Vovô\", \"botafogo\", \"fluminense\"),\n  (\"Clássico dos Gigantes\"? botafogo-vasco \"Clássico da Amizade\"...) — include (\"Botafogo vs Vasco\", ...)? Use \"Clássico da Amizade\", botafogo, vascodagama.\n  (\"Majestoso\", \"corinthians\", \"saopaulo\"),\n  (\"Choque-Rei\", \"palmeiras\", \"saopaulo\"),\n  (\"Derby Paulista\", \"palmeiras\", \"corinthians\"),\n  (\"Grenal\", \"gremio\", \"internacional\"),\n  (\"Clássico Mineiro (Atletibademineirense)\"? just \"Clássico Mineiro\", atleticomineiro, cruzeiro),\n  (\"Ba-Vi\", \"bahia\", \"vitoria\"),\n  (\"Clássico-Rei\"?? ceara-fortaleza is \"Clássico-Rei\", \n  (\"Atletiba\", \"atleticoparanaense\", \"coritiba\"),\n  (\"Re-Pa\", \"remo\", \"paysandu\"),\n  (\"Clássico dos Clássicos\", \"sportrecife\", \"nautico\"),\n  (\"Clássico das Multidões\", \"sportrecife\", \"santacruz\"),\n  (\"San-São\", \"santos\", \"saopaulo\"),\n  (\"Clássico da Vila\"? santos-palmeiras no...\n]\n```\nGood: 15 derbies.\n\n**formatting.py** — render results as text per spec examples:\n- format_matches(result) → \"- 2023-09-03: Flamengo 2-1 Fluminense (Brasileirão Serie A Round 22)\" + \"... (N more matches)\" + h2h summary\n- format_team_stats → Corinthians example format\n- format_players list, format_standings table, etc.\n\n**server.py** — MCPServer with tools wrapping analysis + formatting. ~19 tools. Resource for dataset info. main() → server.run(\"stdio\").\n\nTool naming with docstrings + type hints. Tools return formatted text (str).\n\n**Tests** — pytest-bdd features. conftest.py:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom brazilian_soccer.loader import load_soccer_data\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef soccer_data():\n    return load_soccer_data()   # cwd = repo root when running pytest\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef mcp_server():\n    import server\n    return server.build_server()\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef mcp_tool_caller(mcp_server):  # async runner\n    import asyncio\n    def call(tool, **args):\n        return asyncio.run(_call(mcp_server, tool, args))\n    return call\n```\n\nFor feature files — Gherkin with Given/When/Then matching the spec's own scenarios:\n\nmatch_queries.feature:\n```gherkin\nFeature: Match Queries\n  Scenario: Find matches between two teams\n    Given the match data is loaded\n    When I search for matches between \"Flamengo\" and \"Fluminense\"\n    Then I should receive a list of matches\n    And each match should have date, scores, and competition\n  Scenario: Team matches in a season\n    When I search for matches of team \"Palmeiras\" in season \"2023\"\n    Then all returned matches should be from season 2023\n    And at least 30 matches should be returned\n  Scenario: Matches by date range\n    When I search for matches between \"2023-06-01\" and \"2023-06-30\"\n    Then all returned matches should fall within the date range\n  Scenario: Last match between two teams\n    ...\n```\n\nteam_queries.feature: Corinthians home 2022 record (W/D/L, GF/GA, win rate), most goals scored Serie A 2023, h2h Palmeiras-Santos.\n\nplayer_queries.feature: Brazilian players count>800, top-rated Brazilian (Neymar Jr 92? — verify: top Brazilian overall in FIFA data... Neymar 94? Let me check: earlier sample showed Messi 94; Neymar's overall in FIFA 19 = 92? The spec example says \"Neymar Jr - Overall: 92\". Let me verify in data — I'll write the test to match data reality: assert top Brazilian player is Neymar Jr with overall >= 92), players at a club, forwards from São Paulo FC (hmm — FIFA lacks São Paulo FC! So \"Show me all forwards from São Paulo FC\" → 0... The test should use a club that exists, e.g. forwards from Santos. I'll test with Santos/Grêmio. Also test empty-result messaging for Flamengo (documenting data limitation), Gabriel Barbosa → returns graceful empty/matches \"M. Barbosa\"? Test: searching \"Barbosa\" returns players.\n\ncompetition_queries.feature: 2019 Brasileirão champion = Flamengo ✓ (data supports — Flamengo won 2019), 2018 standings top = Palmeiras ✓, relegated 2020 = Coritiba, Botafogo? Hmm — 2020 relegated: Coritiba, Botafogo, Vasco, Goiás? 2020 Serie A relegated 4: Vasco, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo. Verify from computed standings. Test: relegated list contains Botafogo & Vasco. Copa do Brasil finals listing, Libertadores finals 2019 (Flamengo champion single final). Palmeiras competitions played.\n\nstatistics.feature: avg goals Brasileirão ~2.4, home win rate ~50%, biggest wins (margin >= 6?), best away record team, compare 2018 vs 2019 (champion Palmeiras vs Flamengo).\n\nnormalization.feature: \"Palmeiras-SP\" == \"Palmeiras\" == \"Palmeiras - SP\" key; \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → Corinthians; date formats parse; accents fold \"São Paulo\" == \"Sao Paulo\".\n\nmcp_server.feature: list tools contains expected names; call search_matches tool returns text containing score; call player search; unknown team → friendly error message (isError? ToolError → we raise AnalysisError → tool raises ToolError → CallToolResult with isError? In mcp 2.x, raising ToolError gives error result. Better: tools CATCH AnalysisError and return \"No team found...\" text. Decide: return friendly text, not protocol error.)\n\nperformance.feature: simple lookup < 2s; aggregate (standings + stats) < 5s.\n\nI also want plain unit tests (non-BDD) for normalize edge cases and date parsing — test_unit_normalize.py? Hmm — pytest-bdd features + steps; I'll add test_unit.py for pure functions.\n\n**pytest.ini**:\n```\n[pytest]\nasyncio_mode = strict? (pytest-asyncio 1.4 — mode auto? I'll use asyncio_mode=auto and write async tests without decorators)\ntestpaths = tests\n```\nWith pytest-bdd 8.x: steps files use @given/@when/@then from pytest_bdd. Scenario decorators: @scenario(\"features/x.feature\", \"Scenario name\"). I'll bind via decorators.\n\nTiming: performance tests measure around function calls.\n\n**README.md** — document architecture, tools, how to run server, how to run tests, data decisions (dedup/source selection), name normalization, limitations.\n\n**requirements.txt**:\n```\nmcp>=2.1\npytest>=8\npytest-bdd>=8\npytest-asyncio>=1\n```\n(no pandas needed!)\n\nWait — one design decision to finalize: **asyncio for tool calls in tests** — MCPServer.call_tool is async. In BDD steps (sync), use asyncio.run. The \"when I call the MCP tool\" steps will do asyncio.run(server.call_tool(...)). Need an event loop per call — asyncio.run creates fresh loop each time; fine.\n\nAlso the stdio integration test (true end-to-end): spawn `server.py` via StdioServerParameters + ClientSession... ClientSession API in mcp 2.x: `ClientSession(read_stream, write_stream)` then `await session.initialize()`, `await session.call_tool(...)`. Let me verify ClientSession import & usage quickly when writing the test — I'll write one and adjust. Actually — to reduce risk, I'll write the in-memory tests via server.call_tool (definitely works), PLUS one stdio end-to-end test using the client; if the client API gives trouble I'll iterate. It's valuable to prove `python server.py` actually speaks MCP over stdio. Let me check the ClientSession API surface when I get there.\n\n**One more loader detail** — FIFA CSV has BOM + empty first column name. csv.DictReader with utf-8-sig handles BOM; first column header is '' → row[''] = index. I'll read with DictReader and use row.get('ID') etc. Also fifa columns: 'Jersey Number', 'Preferred Foot', 'Value', 'Wage', 'Height', 'Weight', 'Position', 'Name', 'Age', 'Nationality', 'Overall', 'Potential', 'Club', 'ID'. Values like \"€110.5M\" — parse value/wage to raw strings (keep as-is, or parse to millions? keep raw strings; maybe parse \"€110.5M\" → 110.5 for sorting? Not needed for spec. Keep strings.)\n\nHeight \"5'7\", Weight \"159lbs\" — keep raw strings.\n\nSkills: Crossing..GKReflexes numeric columns → dict of int. Values like \"88+2\" for position-specific ones (LS, ST...) — those have \"+2\" suffixes! The general skills (Crossing etc.) are plain ints. I'll only parse the plain-int columns for skills (Crossing..GKReflexes) with parse_int fallback skip.\n\nPositions: 'RF','ST','LW','GK', etc. For \"forwards\" filter: positions {ST, CF? no CF in data? FIFA 19 positions: RF, ST, LW, LF, CF, RW, LS, RS}. Player Position column values like 'RF'. Forward group = {\"ST\",\"CF\",\"LW\",\"RW\",\"LF\",\"RF\",\"LS\",\"RS\"}. Midfield: CAM, CM, CDM, LM, RM, LAM, RAM, LCM, RCM, LDM, RDM. Defense: LB, RB, CB, LCB, RCB, LWB, RWB. GK. I'll add a `position_group(player)` helper and support position query accepting either exact position (\"LW\") or group (\"forward\", \"midfielder\", \"defender\", \"goalkeeper\"). \n\n**Spec-check: \"At least 20 sample questions can be answered\"** — I'll include in README a table of 25 sample questions mapped to tools, and tests covering many of them.\n\n**\"Cross-file queries work (e.g., player + match data)\"** — team_profile(team) merges match data + player rosters from FIFA (e.g. team_profile(\"Grêmio\") shows matches AND squad from FIFA). I'll add players to team_profile output: \"Squad in FIFA data: N players (top: ...)\". That's the cross-file query ✓. Also search_players club resolution uses same keys ✓.\n\nAlright — also `__init__.py` exports load_soccer_data etc.\n\nOne more thing — **performance of load**: 18k players x 80 cols + ~24k raw match rows x 5 files. csv module: fast enough (~1s). FIFA skills dict per player: 30+ fields x 18k = fine.\n\n**Design the formatted outputs** to match spec examples closely (they're the \"expected answer format\").\n\nNow — WRITE THE CODE. Order:\n1. brazilian_soccer/normalize.py\n2. brazilian_soccer/models.py\n3. brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n4. brazilian_soccer/analysis.py\n5. brazilian_soccer/formatting.py\n6. brazilian_soccer/__init__.py\n7. server.py\n8. Verify with a smoke script on real data (check big-12 club key unification, load report, spot queries)\n9. tests/* \n10. pytest.ini, requirements.txt, README.md\n11. Run tests, fix\n12. Also quick MCP stdio smoke test manually\n\nLet me set up the todo list and go.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c588357001c8YyiuyKyltbrB","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c525fa6001gcZsOdOO5rl0BB"}}
