 wait, \"Semifinal (Round N)\" — is max-1 always semifinal in Copa? From the data: rounds 1-8, round 7 = semifinal (4 matches/season) ✓; but in 2021 the file only has rounds 1-4 → max=4 → round 4 labeled \"Final\" ✗ WRONG (2021 file incomplete — round 4 is round of 16, not final!). Hmm!! The 2021 Copa cup-file has rounds {1:40, 2:20, 3:32, 4:16} — my logic would call round 4 the \"Final\" — bad. But note: for 2021, the chosen source is the cup FILE (92 scored ≥ 80% of brf's 111)... so the unified 2021 Copa has rounds 1-4 only, and rounds 4 matches would be mislabeled \"Final\" ✗.\n\nHow to fix? The \"final\" detection can't be purely data-driven when data is truncated. Options:\n(a) Hard-code known final-round numbers per season for Copa do Brasil: rounds 1-8 with 8=final for 2012-2020; 2021 file truncated (no final). I could define: for the copa file, the final round is 8 (known format), or for brf-source copa seasons (2022/2023) the final is... brf has no round info at all (stage empty)! For brf copa matches, stage = \"\" (no round). Hmm — so for 2022/2023 Copa (brf), how do I find finals? No round/stage info. Ugh.\n\nWait — does brf have round info? Columns: tournament,home,home_goal,away_goal,away,corners...,time,date,ht_diff,at_diff,ht_result,at_result,total_corners. NO round column. So brf-sourced cup matches have no stage. So \"Find all Copa do Brasil finals\" for 2022/2023 — can't identify finals from brf. Hmm.\n\nAlternative approach for cup finals detection: **aggregate-final detection**: for two-legged finals, the final is the LAST pairing of the season chronologically: take the last few matches by date; the final = the pairing(s) among the latest matches. For 2-legged finals: last 2 matches of season involve same two teams (reversed venues). Heuristic: take the latest 2 matchdays... For Libertadores I have stage == \"final\" ✓ explicit. For Copa: rounds exist in the cup file (2012-2020 complete with round 8 = final ✓), 2021 incomplete (max round 4 = round of 16 — NOT final), 2022-2023 brf (no rounds).\n\nPragmatic solution for Copa do Brasil finals:\n- Use rounds when they're trustworthy: label \"Final\" only if the season's round structure looks complete. How to know complete? Copa do Brasil standard format has the champion determined... hmm.\n- Alternative: KNOWN final round = 8 for the cup file (verified: 2012-2020 all have rounds 1-8 with 8=final; only 2021 is truncated at 4). So hardcode: for copa file seasons, final round = 8. If a season lacks round 8 → no final labeled (2021) ✓ correct!\n- For brf copa seasons (2022, 2023): no round info → identify finals by \"latest matches of the season between the same two teams\" heuristic: take matches in the last ~3 distinct dates of the season; the final teams = the pairing that appears in the last 2 distinct dates with reversed venues. Let me check: Copa 2022 final: Flamengo vs Coritiba (Nov 2022, 2 legs: Oct 30 + Nov 6?). Copa 2023: São Paulo vs Flamengo (Sep+Oct 2023... 2023 Copa final: Flamengo vs São Paulo, Sep 24 + Oct 1?? Hmm — 2023 final: São Paulo 1-0 Flamengo? No wait — 2023 Copa do Brasil champion: São Paulo beat Flamengo (1st leg 1-0 Morumbi Sep 24? hmm; 2nd leg 1-1 Maracanã Oct 4?? Something like that). The data check earlier: \"Flamengo-Coritiba 2022\" rows exist? I greped {home,away}=={Flamengo,Coritiba} in Copa 2022 — output showed nothing printed! Let me re-look at that output... The grep printed nothing for Flamengo-Coritiba 2022?? Hmm — wait the output shows no lines between \"Copa 2023 {...}\" and \"Copa early 2022 rows: 55\". So NO Flamengo-Coritiba rows in brf Copa 2022?! But earlier I said 2022 Copa final Flamengo-Coritiba... hmm wait, maybe the 2022 final was Flamengo vs Corinthians?? No no. 2022 Copa do Brasil FINAL: Flamengo x Corinthians! (Flamengo won 2-1 agg? Hmm — actually I recall Flamengo beat Corinthians in the 2022 Copa final — penalty shootout after 1-1 agg? no...). Honestly my memory: 2022 final Flamengo vs Corinthians ✓ (1-1, 1-1, Flamengo on pens? or 2-1?). Whatever — data will tell. Let me implement the generic heuristic and validate against known champions (2012-2020 from round-8 data: Palmeiras 2012, Flamengo 2013, Atlético-MG 2014, Palmeiras 2015, Grêmio 2016, Cruzeiro 2017, Cruzeiro 2018, Athletico-PR 2019, Palmeiras 2020).\n\nLet me make _finalize_stage smarter:\n1. For the cup file source: if round 8 exists in season → final = round 8; round 7 → Semifinal; round 6 → Quarterfinal; round 5 → Round of 16; rounds 1-4 → \"Round N\". (Hardcoded structure for the cup file's known format.)\n2. For brf cup matches (no rounds): heuristic \"last pairing\" — the two teams involved in the season's latest date; label those matches \"Final\". For 2 legs: matches between the final two teams near season end (both venues). Risk: if the last scheduled rows are QF... For 2022 Copa brf: months 09:2, 10:2 — the last 2 matches in October 2022 = final legs?? 2022 Copa final was Oct 27 + Nov 2?? hmm brf Copa 2022 has only 2 October matches — plausibly the 2 final legs! Similarly 2023: 09:2 = 2 matches in Sept 2023 — 2023 final was Sep 24 + Oct 1?? brf Copa 2023 months: 08:2, 09:2. Hmm. Let me check what those last matches are — verify they're finals.\n3. Libertadores: stage field exists ✓ — label finals from stage==\"final\" ✓. Round of 16/quarterfinals/semifinals ✓ title-cased.\n\nLet me restructure: I'll do the stage labeling in a dedicated function per source type, and validate empirically. Simplest correct approach given verified data:\n\nCopa do Brasil (file source): KNOWN_CUP_FINAL_ROUND = 8. Labels: 8→Final, 7→Semifinal, 6→Quarterfinal, 5→Round of 16, else \"Round N\".\nCopa do Brasil (brf source): find the two finalists = teams playing on the last distinct date of the season; final matches = all matches between those two teams in the last 2 distinct dates... or simpler: matches on the last 2 distinct dates involving both finalists. Label \"Final\".\nLibertadores: stage.title() with \"final\"→\"Final\" etc. Already handled by title() — \"group stage\" → \"Group Stage\" ✓, \"round of 16\" → \"Round Of 16\" (meh, \"Round of 16\" nicer — custom map).\n\nLet me also reconsider `_finalize_stage` for leagues: stage = \"Round N\" ✓ fine.\n\nAlso the champion determination for cups: aggregate score over final legs; if aggregate tied → \"decided on penalties (not recorded in data)\" with both finalists listed.\n\nLet me now fix loader.py's stage logic + the _display_for performance bug. I'll rewrite those parts:\n\n- Build `display_map` once: for each key → CLUBS display / FOREIGN display / pick_display(variants). Actually the registry already has display names after build_registry! I can pass `registry` into build_competition and use `registry.teams[key].display`. But careful: registry built from ALL raw names INCLUDING duplicates — display picked from variants ✓. So `_display_for` becomes `registry.get(key).display or fallback`. Much faster: O(1) lookup.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: registry display for canonical clubs = CLUBS display ✓; for uncurated = picked raw variant (e.g. \"Boavista Sport Club\"). \n\nAlso — one more inconsistency: `build_registry` is called BEFORE competitions are built; registry_names includes every raw name (with file labels). Good.\n\nLet me rewrite loader.py sections: replace `_display_for` + `_stage_label` + `_finalize_stage` with registry-based display and proper stage labeling.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c5a8277001bLKlgwpmhBHJT2","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c5a43c8001EpYBksafrI7TI6"}}
