{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788256047367,"sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-aa77ae41b5fd98d7","state":{"status":"error","input":{"filePath":"/Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-7ooj92cj/retort-9b01b8399d96/brazilian_soccer/analysis.py","newString":"POSITION_GROUP_ALIASES = {\n    \"forward\": \"Forward\",\n    \"forwards\": \"Forward\",\n    \"attacker\": \"Forward\",\n    \"striker\": \"Forward\",\n    \"midfielder\": \"Midfielder\",\n    \"midfield\": \"Midfielder\",\n    \"defender\": \"Defender\",\n    \"defense\": \"Defender\",\n    \"goalkeeper\": \"Goalkeeper\",\n    \"goalie\": \"Goalkeeper\",\n    \"gk\": \"Goalkeeper\",\n    \"keeper\": \"Goalkeeper\",\n}\n\n\ndef _position_query_group(position_q: str) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"Map a user-supplied position to a position group, if it is one.\"\"\"\n    return POSITION_GROUP_ALIASES.get(position_q.strip().lower())\n\n\ndef _position_matches(player: Player, position_q: str) -> bool:\n    if player.position.upper() == position_q.upper():\n        return True\n    return fold(player.position_group) == fold(position_q)","oldString":"def _position_matches_unused(): ...\n\n\ndef _position_query_group_unused(): ..."},"error":"Could not find oldString in the file. It must match exactly, including whitespace, indentation, and line endings.","time":{"start":1788256047348,"end":1788256047365}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Almost everything works. Issues found:\n\n1. **\"search_players club=Santos position=forward\" returns nothing** — Santos players exist (20 Brazilians at Santos), but position group matching fails! Why? `position_matches`: `player.position.upper() == exact_position` (exact_position = \"FORWARD\" — no) or `fold(player.position_group) == position_group_q` where position_group_q = position_group(\"forward\") = \"Unknown\"!! My `position_group()` function only maps exact FIFA position codes — \"forward\" isn't in the sets → \"Unknown\". And the guard `and position_group_q != \"Unknown\"` makes it never match. BUG: I need to map the user's group word (\"forward\") to a group name. Fix: translate the query first: if query word in {forward, attacker, striker...} → \"Forward\"; midfielder/midfield → \"Midfielder\"; defender/defense → \"Defender\"; goalkeeper/goalie/gk → \"Goalkeeper\". Then compare `player.position_group == group`.\n\n2. **\"Flamenguinho\" resolves to Flamengo** — fuzzy matching is TOO aggressive: \"Flamenguinho\" vs \"Flamengo\"? SequenceMatcher ratio... \"flamenguinho\" (12 chars) vs \"flamengo\" (8): ratio = 2*8/(12+8)? Common subsequence ~8 → 0.8 ≥ 0.75 → matches. It returns Flamengo results without flagging uncertainty. Hmm — is that bad? For a demo, lenient matching is nice, but surprising. Better: fuzzy threshold higher (0.85?) AND prefer exact/startswith/contains strongly. \"Flamenguinho\" contains \"flamengo\" as substring! `qc in n` → contains match ✓ — that's why it matched (contains rule), not fuzzy. That's by design (\"palmeiras\" ⊂ \"palmeirass\"... wait no, that's `n in qc`). Contains-both-ways is lenient. It's fine for a demo server — it found the right team. I'll leave as is (reasonable behavior: Flamenguinho → Flamengo). Actually hmm — could mislead: \"Santos FC\" → contains \"santos\" ✓ correct. \"Internacional\" vs \"Internacional-SC\"?? query \"internacional\" → exact key match → RS club ✓. OK keep.\n\n3. **Palmeiras 2023 Brasileirão = 37 matches** (19+10+8, 61 GF) — real 2023: Palmeiras played 38 (20W 8D 10L 63 pts? actually Palmeiras 2023: 38 matches). Our brf 2023 Serie A = 377 matches (3 missing) → Palmeiras got 37. Data reality — acceptable, and standings notes flag incompleteness. Fine.\n\n4. **Libertadores 2013 final: Olimpia 2-0 Atlético-MG; Atlético-MG 2-0 Olimpia — level on aggregate** ✓ (real: Atlético won on penalties ✓ our note says exactly that ✓✓).\n\n5. **competition_stats Brasileirão: 49.7% home wins, avg 2.57 goals** — matches the spec example ballpark (\"2.47 avg, 47.3% home\" — different era data, fine).\n\n6. Grêmio team_profile shows Serie B 2022 ✓ (Grêmio was relegated 2021, played Serie B 2022, promoted ✓✓) — the unified data gets relegation stories right!\n\nNow fix the position group matching bug in analysis.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c5dcc6f001s5313xaws7dROk","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c5dafee001S4waianXIqM18l"}}
