pa finals count\ncf = an.competition_finals(data, \"Copa do Brasil\")\nprint(\"Copa finals:\", [(f.season, f.winner_display) for f in cf])\n\n# 5. Palmeiras competitions\nprof = an.team_profile(data, \"Palmeiras\")\nprint(\"Palmeiras comps:\", [e.competition_id for e in prof.entries])\n\n# 6. h2h totals\nh = an.head_to_head(data, \"Flamengo\", \"Fluminense\")\nprint(\"Fla-Flu:\", h.total, h.wins_a, h.wins_b, h.draws)\n\n# 7. biggest win margins\nbw = an.biggest_wins(data, limit=5)\nprint(\"biggest margins:\", [(m.margin, m.score) for m in bw])\n\n# 8. 2018 champion + relegated\nt18, _ = an.standings(data, \"brasileirao\", 2018)\nprint(\"2018 champ:\", t18.champion.team, t18.champion.points)\nprint(\"2018 relegated:\", [r.team for r in t18.relegated])\n\n# 9. derbies count 2023\nd = an.derbies(data, season=2023)\nprint(\"2023 derbies:\", [(x.label, len(x.matches)) for x in d])\n\n# 10. Brasileirão 2023 standings champ\nt23, n23 = an.standings(data, \"brasileirao\", 2023)\nprint(\"2023 champ:\", t23.champion.team, t23.champion.points, \"| notes:\", n23)\nprint(\"2023 relegated:\", [r.team for r in t23.relegated])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788256105919,"end":1788256105942}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"All player queries work correctly now. The FIFA data has fake players for Brazilian clubs (FIFA 19 didn't license Brazilian squads — names like \"Luimo Boas Santos\" are generated), but that's the data we have; the tools work correctly over it.\n\nNow let me write the BDD test suite. Structure:\n\n```\ntests/\n  conftest.py          — session fixtures: soccer_data, mcp server, call helper\n  features/\n    match_queries.feature\n    team_queries.feature\n    player_queries.feature\n    competition_queries.feature\n    statistics.feature\n    normalization.feature\n    mcp_server.feature\n    performance.feature\n  test_match_queries.py\n  test_team_queries.py\n  test_player_queries.py\n  test_competition_queries.py\n  test_statistics.py\n  test_normalization.py     (also plain unit tests for normalize + loader parsing)\n  test_mcp_server.py        (in-memory + stdio end-to-end)\n  test_performance.py\n```\n\npytest-bdd 8.x usage:\n```python\nfrom pytest_bdd import scenarios, given, when, then, parsers\n\nscenarios(\"features/match_queries.feature\")\n\n@when(parsers.parse('I search for matches between \"{team_a}\" and \"{team_b}\"'))\ndef search_between(team_a, team_b, context):\n    ...\n```\n\npytest-bdd 8 needs fixtures for sharing state — use a `context` dict fixture.\n\nFor the MCP server tests in BDD steps: asyncio.run for call_tool.\n\nLet me now write the feature files carefully — they must align with verified data facts:\n\n**match_queries.feature**:\n- Scenario: Find matches between two teams (Flamengo/Fluminense) — expect list with date/score/competition, total ≥ 30 matches (44 verified).\n- Scenario: Matches by season — Palmeiras 2023 ≥ 30 (43 verified).\n- Scenario: Date range filter — all matches between 2023-06-01 and 2023-06-15.\n- Scenario: Last match between teams — Flamengo vs Corinthians most recent.\n- Scenario: Match has required fields (date, home, away, goals, competition).\n\n**team_queries.feature**:\n- Corinthians 2022 stats — verified: 58 matches overall... wait — the spec example asks \"What is Corinthians' home record in 2022?\" — team_stats returns home record: 29 matches 18W 7D 4L GF 41 GA 15 ✓ (verified numbers above). Hmm wait — Corinthians 2022 = 58 total matches? Brasileirão 38 + Copa do Brasil 2022 (Corinthians played to final! ~8 matches) + Libertadores 2022 (Corinthians played Libertadores 2022 ✓ group + R16 = 8 matches). 38+8+8=54... plus Sudamericana? not in data. 58 — fine, all comps.\n  Wait — the spec's example says \"Corinthians home record (2022 Brasileirão): Matches: 19...\" — real Corinthians 2022 Brasileirão home: 19 games. Our data: 29 home matches across all comps. With competition=\"Brasileirão\", season=2022: 19 home. I'll test that.\n- Top-scoring team Serie A 2023 — verified: standings show goals. Actually \"Which team scored the most goals in Serie A 2023?\" — from compare test: top scoring 2023? Let me compute in test via standings rows (Palmeiras 61? Botafogo? Botafogo 2023 scored 66? I don't know — the test will compute from our standings function and assert it's the argmax — that's circular. Better: assert the top-scoring team has more goals than Palmeiras's 61 and that it's in a known set {Palmeiras, Botafogo, Flamengo, Atlético-MG}? Hmm — circular tests are weak but BDD on real data needs anchors. I'll assert top team goals >= 60 and its goals ≥ every other team's. That validates internal consistency.\n- Palmeiras vs Santos h2h: verified 17W-16W-8D, 41 matches ✓ assert totals sum.\n\n**player_queries.feature**:\n- Brazilian players: 827 total ≥ 800.\n- Top-rated Brazilian = Neymar Jr 92 ✓ (also matches spec example!).\n- Players at Fluminense = 20 (FIFA).\n- Forwards from Santos ≥ 3 (3 verified — hmm 3 exactly? \"9 players in dataset\" before fix... after fix 3 (Luimo, Arthur, Fachendinho — ST only... wait \"forwards\" would include ST/LS/RS... the earlier run with substring showed 9 including Laguna; the exact-key version shows 3 — all ST. OK assert ≥ 3.)\n- Gabriel Barbosa lookup → no players... spec says \"Who is Gabriel Barbosa?\" → search FIFA by name. Data has no Gabriel Barbosa. Test: player_details(\"Gabriel Barbosa\") raises AnalysisError (no match) OR search_players(name=\"Barbosa\") returns ≥ 1 (M. Barbosa, Hélder Barbosa ✓). I'll test search by name \"Barbosa\" returns players ✓ and document.\n- Player details Neymar: overall 92, position LW, club PSG.\n\n**competition_queries.feature**:\n- 2019 Brasileirão champion Flamengo with 90 pts ✓.\n- 2018 standings top = Palmeiras ✓ (80 pts verified).\n- 2020 relegated: bottom 4 — real: Vasco, Goiás, Coritiba, Botafogo. Let me verify from our table (2020 standings printed earlier only top 9). I'll assert relegated contains Botafogo and Vasco (verify before finalizing test!). Let me check.\n- Copa do Brasil 2019 champion: Athletico Paranaense ✓ verified.\n- Libertadores finals listed for seasons 2013-2020 (finals missing 2021; 2022 broken NA row dropped) — assert at least 6 finals, and 2019 final winner Flamengo.\n- Palmeiras competitions: at least {brasileirao, copadobrasil, libertadores} ✓.\n\n**statistics.feature**:\n- Avg goals Brasileirão between 2.0 and 3.0 (2.57 ✓).\n- Home win rate > away win rate ✓.\n- Biggest wins margin ≥ 6.\n- Best away record 2023 = Flamengo ✓ verified.\n- Compare 2018/2019: champions Palmeiras/Flamengo ✓.\n\n**normalization.feature**:\n- \"Palmeiras-SP\" == \"Palmeiras\" == \"Palmeiras - SP\" → same key.\n- \"Sport Club Corinthians Paulista\" → corinthians key.\n- \"Atletico-MG\" == \"Atlético - MG\" == \"Atlético Mineiro\" → atleticomineiro.\n- \"Athletico\" (bare) → atleticoparanaense; \"Atletico-PR\" same key.\n- \"São Paulo\" == \"Sao Paulo\" == \"Sao Paulo-SP\".\n- Date formats: \"2023-09-24\", \"29/03/2003\", \"2012-05-19 18:30:00\" all parse.\n- Namesake disambiguation: \"Flamengo - PI\" ≠ \"flamengo\".\n- FIFA club \"América FC (Minas Gerais)\" → americamineiro.\n- UTF-8: \"Grêmio\" == \"Gremio\".\n\n**mcp_server.feature**:\n- Server lists 18 tools including required names.\n- call search_matches returns text containing \"Flamengo\" and a score pattern.\n- call search_players returns Neymar.\n- Unknown team → friendly \"Could not answer\" text, not an exception.\n- stdio end-to-end: launch server.py subprocess via client, initialize, call a tool. (Separate async test — not in feature file; or as scenario steps using asyncio.run inside steps. I'll do a feature with a scenario that spawns the subprocess.)\n\nHmm — stdio test runtime: server startup ~2s (load data). OK.\n\n**performance.feature**:\n- search_matches simple < 2s ✓ (0.06s).\n- standings + team_stats aggregate < 5s ✓ (0.001s).\n\nAlso plain unit tests: parse_date formats, parse_int NA handling, team_key edge cases, position_group, rebucket season logic. I'll fold most into test_normalization.py + a small test_loader_units.py. Actually — keep it in the features where sensible + one test_unit.py.\n\nWait — one concern about pytest-bdd scenarios + the parser: I'll use `parsers.parse` with quotes. And \"Given the match data is loaded\" shared across features — steps defined per test module (pytest-bdd requires steps defined in the module that binds scenarios, or in conftest via fixtures... steps can be defined in conftest.py? pytest-bdd steps are just fixtures — they CAN live in conftest.py and be shared across feature test modules ✓. I'll put common steps (given data loaded, when call tool X) in conftest.py to avoid duplication, with a `ctx` fixture for state.\n\nDesign conftest.py:\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nimport pytest\nfrom brazilian_soccer.loader import load_soccer_data\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef soccer_data():\n    return load_soccer_data()\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")\ndef mcp_server():\n    import server\n    return server.build_server()\n\ndef run_async(coro):\n    return asyncio.run(coro)\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef ctx():\n    return {}\n\n# shared BDD steps in conftest:\nfrom pytest_bdd import given, when, then, parsers\n\n@given(\"the match data is loaded\", target_fixture=\"data_loaded\")\ndef data_loaded(soccer_data):\n    return soccer_data\n```\n\nHmm — pytest-bdd steps need to reference fixtures. Since steps are fixtures themselves, they can take other fixtures as args ✓.\n\nFor MCP tool call step: \n```python\n@when(parsers.parse('I call the MCP tool \"{tool}\" with'))\ndef call_tool(mcp_server, ctx, tool, datatable??)\n```\n datatable handling in pytest-bdd: `@when(parsers.parse(...), target_fixture=\"result\")` with a step table? pytest-bdd supports step arguments via `fixture` named after the step? Actually pytest-bdd 8: steps receive the parsed args; for data tables there's `context`... pytest-bdd doesn't support Gherkin data tables natively (it has parsers). I'll keep scenarios with quoted params only.\n\nPlan for MCP-level scenarios (feature mcp_server.feature):\n```gherkin\nScenario: List available tools\n  Given the MCP server is running\n  When I list the available tools\n  Then at least 15 tools should be available\n  And the tools should include \"search_matches\" and \"search_players\"\n\nScenario: Answer a match question through the server\n  Given the MCP server is running\n  When I call the tool \"search_matches\" with team \"Flamengo\" and opponent \"Fluminense\"\n  Then the response should contain \"Flamengo\"\n  And the response should contain \"head\"  -- hmm\n```\n\nHmm — step \"When I call the tool \"search_matches\" with team \"Flamengo\" and opponent \"Fluminense\"\" — I'd need per-tool steps. Simpler: a generic step that takes tool + kwargs via a simple mini-DSL: `When I call the tool \"search_matches\" with arguments \"team=Flamengo, opponent=Fluminense\"`. Parse \"k=v, k2=v2\" in the step. Values: try int conversion. That's flexible and testable. \n\nThen most BDD scenarios can go through the MCP layer — nice end-to-end coverage. But some assertions need structured data (e.g. \"each match has date, scores, competition\") — for those, use the analysis layer directly via python steps. I'll mix: features use the MCP text layer for output-format scenarios and analysis layer for structural scenarios.\n\nLet me now verify a few more facts before writing tests:\n1. 2020 relegated teams from our standings.\n2. Corinthians home record 2022 Brasileirão (19 matches, expect 11W? let me compute — real 2022 Corinthians home: 19 games. I'll assert matches == 19 and W+D+L == 19 without pinning exact W).\n3. Libertadores finals count by season.\n4. \"Palmeiras played in\" competitions set.\n5. Flamengo vs Fluminense total (44).\n\nLet me run these checks, then write all test files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05c5ea572001HyblrYgqR4EvmT","sessionID":"ses_fa3ba9cb2ffeFGH9rzdcVMfYFE","messageID":"msg_05c5e57ef001fOgFThSEZleHE1"}}
